window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-phy-fluids","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids · Practice","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In a hydraulic lift, the small piston has a radius of 2.0 cm and the large piston has a radius of 10 cm. A force of 200 N is applied on the small piston. What maximum load can the large piston support? (Ignore the weights of the pistons and atmospheric pressure.)","image":"","options":["1000 N","5000 N","40 N","25000 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $r_1$ = 2.0 cm, $r_2$ = 10 cm, $F_1$ = 200 N.\nFormula: By Pascal's law the pressure is transmitted undiminished, so $F_2 = F_1\\frac{A_2}{A_1} = F_1\\left(\\frac{r_2}{r_1}\\right)^2$.\nSubstituting: $F_2 = 200 \\times (10/2)^2 = 200 \\times 25$.\nSo $F_2$ = 5000 N. Using the ratio of radii instead of areas would wrongly give 1000 N.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A large tank open to the atmosphere is filled with water. A small hole of area 2.0 cm$^2$ is made in its side wall 5.0 m below the free surface. The hole's area is negligible compared with the tank's cross-section. What is the initial volume of water flowing out per second? (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["1.0 L/s","1.4 L/s","2.0 L/s","20 L/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: h = 5.0 m, A = 2.0 cm$^2$ = $2.0\\times10^{-4}$ m$^2$, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: By Torricelli's law $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$, and the volume flow rate is Q = Av.\nSubstituting: $v = \\sqrt{2\\times10\\times5} = 10$ m/s, so $Q = 2.0\\times10^{-4}\\times10 = 2.0\\times10^{-3}$ m$^3$/s.\nSo Q = 2.0 L/s. Using $v = \\sqrt{gh}$ by mistake would give about 1.4 L/s.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Pressure\n(B) Density\n(C) Relative density\n(D) Product $gh$ (g = acceleration due to gravity, h = height)\nList-II:\n(I) Dimensionless\n(II) $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$\n(III) $[L^{2}T^{-2}]$\n(IV) $[ML^{-3}]$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Pressure is force per area: $[MLT^{-2}]/[L^2] = [ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$, so A-II.\n(B) Density is mass per volume: $[ML^{-3}]$, so B-IV.\n(C) Relative density is a ratio of two densities, so it is dimensionless: C-I.\n(D) $gh$ has dimensions $[LT^{-2}][L] = [L^2T^{-2}]$, so D-III.\nCheck: multiplying (D) by density gives $\\rho g h$, which has the dimensions of pressure, as it should.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A metal block of base area 0.050 m$^2$ lies on a horizontal table, separated from it by a liquid film 0.40 mm thick. A string attached to the block passes over an ideal pulley and carries a hanging mass of 0.020 kg. After release, the block slides with a constant speed of 0.20 m/s. Find the coefficient of viscosity of the liquid. (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["0.008 Pa s","0.08 Pa s","0.0032 Pa s","0.002 Pa s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 0.020 kg, A = 0.050 m$^2$, $l = 4\\times10^{-4}$ m, v = 0.20 m/s.\nThe speed is constant, so the shear force equals the tension: $F = mg = 0.20$ N.\nShear stress = $\\frac{F}{A} = 4$ Pa. Strain rate = $\\frac{v}{l} = 500\\ \\text{s}^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\eta = \\frac{F/A}{v/l} = \\frac{4}{500}$.\nSo $\\eta$ = 0.008 Pa s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about fluid flow are correct?\n(i) In a steady flow, the velocity of the fluid at any given point does not change with time.\n(ii) In a steady flow, every fluid particle has the same velocity at all points of the flow.\n(iii) In a region where streamlines are crowded closer together, the flow speed is smaller.\n(iv) When the flow speed exceeds a limiting value called the critical speed, the flow loses its steadiness and becomes turbulent.\n(v) For an incompressible fluid, the relation $Av = $ constant expresses conservation of mass.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the definition of steady flow.\n(ii) Incorrect: velocity can differ from point to point. A particle may speed up or slow down as it moves along its path.\n(iii) Incorrect: crowded streamlines mark a narrower cross-section, so by $Av$ = constant the speed is larger there.\n(iv) Correct: above the critical speed, the flow becomes turbulent.\n(v) Correct: the continuity equation follows from equal mass flowing in and out per unit time.\nCorrect statements: (i), (iv) and (v), so the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A soap film is formed on a U-shaped wire frame closed by a light sliding bar of length 10 cm. The slider stays in equilibrium when a force of $1.46 \\times 10^{-2}$ N pulls it outward. What is the surface tension of the soap solution?","image":"","options":["0.073 N/m","0.146 N/m","0.0365 N/m","0.73 N/m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: F = $1.46 \\times 10^{-2}$ N and l = 0.10 m.\nFormula: the film has two surfaces, so $F = 2Sl$, which gives $S = \\frac{F}{2l}$.\nSubstituting: $S = \\frac{1.46 \\times 10^{-2}}{2 \\times 0.10}$.\nSo S = 0.073 N/m. Forgetting the factor 2 gives the wrong value 0.146 N/m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Surface tension can be expressed either as force per unit length or as surface energy per unit area. What are its dimensions?","image":"","options":["$[MLT^{-2}]$","$[MT^{-2}]$","$[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$","$[MT^{-1}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"As force per unit length: $\\frac{[MLT^{-2}]}{[L]} = [MT^{-2}]$.\nAs energy per unit area: $\\frac{[ML^2T^{-2}]}{[L^2]} = [MT^{-2}]$.\nBoth routes agree, which confirms that N/m and J/m² are the same unit.\n$[MLT^{-2}]$ is the dimension of force, and $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$ is the dimension of pressure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For steady flow of a fluid whose density changes from point to point, the quantity $\\rho A v$ remains the same at every cross-section of a tube of flow.\nStatement II: In a region where the streamlines are closely spaced, the speed of an incompressible fluid is smaller than where the streamlines are far apart.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Mass entering equals mass leaving, so $\\rho_P A_P v_P = \\rho_R A_R v_R$. This general form holds even when the density varies. It reduces to $Av$ = constant only for an incompressible fluid.\nStatement II is false. Closely spaced streamlines mean a smaller cross-section, so $v$ is larger there, not smaller.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pressure unit) with List-II (approximate value in pascal).\nList-I:\n(A) 1 bar\n(B) 1 torr\n(C) 1 atm\n(D) 1 millibar\nList-II:\n(P) 133 Pa\n(Q) $1.013 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa\n(R) $10^{2}$ Pa\n(S) $10^{5}$ Pa","image":"","options":["A-S, B-P, C-Q, D-R","A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R","A-S, B-R, C-Q, D-P","A-R, B-P, C-Q, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) 1 bar = $10^5$ Pa, so A-S.\n(B) 1 torr is the pressure of 1 mm of Hg, which is 133 Pa, so B-P.\n(C) 1 atm is the sea-level atmospheric pressure, $1.013 \\times 10^5$ Pa, so C-Q.\n(D) 1 millibar = $10^{-3}$ bar = $10^2$ Pa, so D-R.\nOption (2) wrongly swaps bar and atm. These two are close in value but not equal.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"For a liquid drop resting on a solid in air, equilibrium at the line of contact gives $S_{la}\\cos\\theta + S_{sl} = S_{sa}$. Which condition must hold for the angle of contact θ to be obtuse, as for mercury on glass?","image":"","options":["$S_{sl} < S_{sa}$","$S_{sl} > S_{sa}$","$S_{la} > S_{sa}$","$S_{sl} = S_{sa}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rearranging the condition: $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{S_{sa} - S_{sl}}{S_{la}}$.\n$S_{la}$ is always positive, so θ is obtuse ($\\cos\\theta < 0$) exactly when $S_{sa} - S_{sl} < 0$, i.e. when $S_{sl} > S_{sa}$.\nIf $S_{sl} < S_{sa}$, then cos θ is positive and θ is acute, which means wetting.\nIf $S_{sl} = S_{sa}$, then θ = 90°.\nThe value of $S_{la}$ alone cannot fix the sign of cos θ.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Atmospheric pressure at the surface of a lake is $1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa. At what depth below the surface will the absolute pressure be $3.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa? (Density of water = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, g = 10 m s$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["20 m","30 m","10 m","200 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P = 3.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, $P_a = 1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, $\\rho$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$.\nFormula: $P = P_a + \\rho g h$, so $h = \\frac{P - P_a}{\\rho g}$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{2.0 \\times 10^{5}}{1000 \\times 10}$.\nSo h = 20 m. The answer 30 m comes from wrongly using the full $3.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa instead of the excess over atmospheric pressure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a small sphere falling through a viscous liquid under Stokes' law. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The viscous force on the sphere acts opposite to its velocity.\n(ii) The terminal velocity does not depend on the density of the liquid.\n(iii) The terminal velocity is proportional to the square of the radius of the sphere.\n(iv) At terminal velocity, the viscous force alone equals the weight of the sphere.\n(v) The terminal velocity is inversely proportional to the coefficient of viscosity of the liquid.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Stokes' drag always opposes the motion.\n(ii) Incorrect: $v_t$ contains $(\\rho-\\sigma)$, where $\\sigma$ is the liquid's density.\n(iii) Correct: $v_t \\propto a^2$.\n(iv) Incorrect: at terminal velocity, viscous force plus buoyant force equals the weight.\n(v) Correct: $v_t \\propto \\frac{1}{\\eta}$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about Bernoulli's principle is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["If viscous drag acts on a flowing liquid, the pressure at the downstream point is lower than the value predicted by Bernoulli's equation","In a pipe of uniform cross-section inclined upward, the flow speed is the same everywhere, so the pressure of an ideal liquid stays constant along the pipe","Bernoulli's equation fails for turbulent flow because velocity and pressure keep fluctuating in time","The kinetic energy lost to internal friction in a real fluid appears as heat, which Bernoulli's equation does not account for"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: work done against the dissipative viscous force lowers $P_2$ below the ideal value.\n(2) Incorrect: continuity keeps $v$ constant, but the height term changes. From $P + \\rho g h = \\text{constant}$, pressure falls by $\\rho g \\Delta h$ as the liquid rises.\n(3) Correct: the equation needs steady flow.\n(4) Correct: viscosity converts kinetic energy into heat, and the ideal equation ignores this loss.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The coefficient of viscosity of a liquid decreases as its temperature rises.\nStatement II: The coefficient of viscosity of a gas decreases as its temperature rises.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. On heating, liquid molecules become more mobile, so $\\eta$ falls. For example, water's viscosity is 1.0 mPl at 20 °C and 0.3 mPl at 100 °C.\nStatement II is incorrect. In a gas, heating increases the random molecular motion, so $\\eta$ increases. Air's viscosity rises from 0.017 mPl at 0 °C to 0.019 mPl at 40 °C.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A strip of paper is held horizontally, hanging down limply from the fingers. When you blow steadily over its upper surface, the strip rises. Which statement correctly explains this?","image":"","options":["The air jet pushes the paper upward by striking it from above","Air above the paper moves faster, so the pressure above becomes lower than the pressure below","Air above the paper moves faster, so the pressure above becomes higher than the pressure below","Blowing makes the air above the paper denser, which lifts the paper"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By Bernoulli's principle along roughly horizontal streamlines, $P + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2$ is constant.\nBlowing increases v above the paper, so the pressure above drops below the still-air (atmospheric) pressure underneath.\nThe pressure difference gives a net upward force, so the paper rises.\nOption (3) reverses the relation: higher speed means lower pressure, not higher.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For two points in a steady flow where the atmospheric pressure is the same, using gauge pressures instead of absolute pressures in Bernoulli's equation gives the same result.\nReason (R): Absolute pressure equals gauge pressure plus atmospheric pressure, so the atmospheric pressure term appears equally on both sides of Bernoulli's equation and cancels.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Bernoulli's equation involves only the pressure difference $P_1 - P_2$, and this difference is unchanged whichever reference is used, provided $P_a$ is the same at both points.\nR is true. Writing $P = P_g + P_a$ on both sides makes $P_a$ cancel.\nThis cancellation is exactly why A holds, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The force that a liquid at rest exerts on the surface of a submerged object is always perpendicular to that surface.\nReason (R): If the force had a component parallel to the surface, then by Newton's third law the object would push the liquid along the surface and the liquid would flow, which cannot happen in a liquid at rest.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: a fluid at rest pushes normally on every surface it touches.\nR is true: suppose the fluid exerted a tangential force on the object. By Newton's third law, the object would exert an equal and opposite tangential force on the fluid. Fluids give way to even a tiny shear force, so the fluid would start flowing.\nA fluid at rest cannot be flowing, so a tangential component is impossible. This is exactly why A holds, so R is the correct explanation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"An aircraft of mass $1.8 \\times 10^5$ kg is in level flight at 720 km/h. Its total wing area is 400 m². The density of air is 1.2 kg/m³ and $g = 10\\ m/s^2$. Ignore the small height difference between the upper and lower wing surfaces. Taking the average air speed as the flight speed, by roughly what percentage must the air speed over the upper surface exceed that over the lower surface?","image":"","options":["9.4 %","4.7 %","18.8 %","0.94 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 1.8 × 10⁵ kg, A = 400 m², ρ = 1.2 kg/m³, and $v_{av}$ = 720 km/h = 200 m/s.\nIn level flight the lift balances the weight, so $\\Delta P = \\frac{mg}{A} = \\frac{1.8 \\times 10^6}{400}$ = 4500 Pa.\nFormula: $\\Delta P = \\frac{\\rho}{2}(v_2^2 - v_1^2) = \\rho v_{av}(v_2 - v_1)$, so $\\frac{v_2 - v_1}{v_{av}} = \\frac{\\Delta P}{\\rho v_{av}^2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{4500}{1.2 \\times 200^2} = \\frac{4500}{48000}$ = 0.094.\nSo the air above the wing must be about 9.4 % faster.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"An open-tube mercury manometer is connected to a gas tank. The mercury in the open arm stands 15.0 cm higher than in the arm joined to the tank. Take atmospheric pressure = $1.01 \\times 10^5$ Pa, density of mercury = $13.6 \\times 10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$ and g = 10 m/s$^2$. What is the absolute pressure of the gas?","image":"","options":["121.4 kPa","20.4 kPa","80.6 kPa","101.0 kPa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 0.15 m, $\\rho = 13.6 \\times 10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$, $P_a = 1.01 \\times 10^5$ Pa.\nFormula: the open arm is higher, so the gas is above atmospheric pressure and $P = P_a + \\rho g h$.\nGauge part: $\\rho g h = 13600 \\times 10 \\times 0.15 = 20400$ Pa.\nSubstituting: $P = 101000 + 20400 = 121400$ Pa = 121.4 kPa.\n20.4 kPa is only the gauge pressure. 80.6 kPa would be the answer if the open arm were lower.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Water poured on a fabric treated with a waterproofing agent forms beads instead of soaking in.\nReason (R): Waterproofing agents create a large angle of contact between water and the fibres.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Treated fabric is not wetted, so water collects as drops.\nR is true. NCERT states that waterproofing agents are added to create a large angle of contact between water and fibres.\nWhen the contact angle is large (obtuse), water molecules attract each other strongly and the fibres only weakly. Creating a liquid–solid surface then costs a lot of energy, so the water stays in beads.\nTherefore R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Two open vessels A and B have equal base areas and are filled with water to the same height. Vessel B is a straight vertical cylinder. Vessel A widens upward like an inverted truncated cone, so it holds much more water. Which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["The force of water on the base is the same in both vessels, and the weight of water in A is greater than this force","The force of water on the base of A is greater, because A holds more water","The pressure at the base of A is greater than that at the base of B","In each vessel, the force of water on the base equals the weight of water in that vessel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The pressure at the base, $P_a + \\rho g h$, depends only on the height h, not on the shape. So the base pressures are equal, and with equal base areas the base forces are equal. This rules out (2) and (3).\nThis is the hydrostatic paradox.\nIn the cylinder B, the base force equals the weight of the water. In A, the water is heavier than the base force: the slanted walls push on the water with normal forces that have an upward component, and this supports the extra water. So (4) fails for A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The coefficient of viscosity of a gas increases when its temperature is raised.\nReason (R): A rise in temperature increases the random motion of the gas molecules.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Table 9.2 shows the viscosity of air rising from 0.017 mPl at 0 °C to 0.019 mPl at 40 °C.\nR is true. Heating increases the random thermal motion of gas molecules.\nNCERT attributes the increase in $\\eta$ for gases to this greater random motion, so R correctly explains A.\nIn liquids the trend is reversed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In the NCERT proof of Pascal's law, a small element of fluid at rest is taken in the shape of a right-angled prism ABC-DEF. Why is this element taken to be very small?","image":"","options":["So that every part of it is at practically the same depth, and the effect of gravity is the same at all its points","So that the surrounding fluid exerts no force on its faces","So that viscous forces on its faces become zero","So that atmospheric pressure does not act on it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The proof compares the pressures on the three faces of the prism.\nIf the element is very small, all its parts are at essentially the same depth below the surface. The effect of gravity is then the same everywhere on it and can be ignored in the force balance.\nOption (2) fails because the surrounding fluid does push on every face, normally to it. These normal forces are exactly what the proof balances.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Two solid steel balls of radii $r$ and $2r$ are dropped into the same long column of glycerine.\nStatement I: The terminal velocity of the larger ball is four times that of the smaller ball.\nStatement II: The viscous drag on a sphere is proportional to the product of its radius and its velocity, while the net downward force (weight minus buoyancy) is proportional to the cube of its radius.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. Stokes' law gives the drag as $F = 6\\pi\\eta a v$, so $F \\propto a v$. The net downward force is $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi a^3(\\rho - \\sigma)g$, so it is proportional to $a^3$.\nAt terminal velocity these two forces balance, giving $v_t = \\frac{2a^2(\\rho-\\sigma)g}{9\\eta}$, so $v_t \\propto a^2$.\nStatement I is correct. Doubling the radius multiplies $v_t$ by $2^2 = 4$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Water flows steadily through a horizontal pipe. The pipe narrows from a cross-section of $4.0\\ cm^2$ to $1.0\\ cm^2$. The speed in the wider part is 1.0 m/s. Treat water as ideal (density $1000\\ kg\\ m^{-3}$). What is the pressure in the wider part minus that in the narrower part?","image":"","options":["7500 Pa","8000 Pa","15000 Pa","3750 Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A_1 = 4.0\\ cm^2$, $A_2 = 1.0\\ cm^2$, $v_1 = 1.0$ m/s, $\\rho = 1000\\ kg\\ m^{-3}$.\nContinuity: $v_2 = \\frac{A_1 v_1}{A_2} = 4.0$ m/s.\nBernoulli for a horizontal pipe: $P_1 - P_2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho(v_2^2 - v_1^2)$.\nSubstituting: $P_1 - P_2 = 0.5 \\times 1000 \\times (16 - 1) = 7500$ Pa.\nSo the pressure difference = 7500 Pa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (physical quantity) with List-II (dimensional formula).\nList-I:\n(A) Pressure\n(B) Coefficient of viscosity\n(C) Surface tension\n(D) Density\nList-II:\n(I) $[MT^{-2}]$\n(II) $[ML^{-3}]$\n(III) $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$\n(IV) $[ML^{-1}T^{-1}]$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Pressure = force/area gives $[MLT^{-2}]/[L^2] = [ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$, so A-III.\n(B) $\\eta$ = shear stress/strain rate gives $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]/[T^{-1}] = [ML^{-1}T^{-1}]$, so B-IV.\n(C) Surface tension = force/length gives $[MLT^{-2}]/[L] = [MT^{-2}]$, so C-I.\n(D) Density = mass/volume gives $[ML^{-3}]$, so D-II.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When three vessels of very different shapes are connected at the bottom by a horizontal pipe and filled with water, the water stands at the same height in all three, even though they hold different amounts of water.\nReason (R): The pressure at a point in a liquid at rest depends on the vertical height of the liquid above it, and not on the cross-sectional area or shape of the container.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. This is the hydrostatic paradox: the water levels are equal although the volumes differ.\nR is true. From $P = P_a + \\rho g h$, the area and shape of the container do not appear.\nR explains A. The bottom pipe is a horizontal plane, so the pressure must be the same below each vessel. This requires the same water height h in all three.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Bernoulli's equation gives the same results whether gauge pressures or absolute pressures are used, provided the same type is used at both points.\nReason (R): Absolute pressure and gauge pressure differ by the atmospheric pressure $P_a$, a constant that appears on both sides of the equation and cancels.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $P = P_a + P_g$, so absolute and gauge pressures differ by $P_a$.\nA is true: writing $P_1 = P_a + P_{g1}$ and $P_2 = P_a + P_{g2}$ in Bernoulli's equation, $P_a$ appears on both sides and cancels.\nOnly pressure differences enter the physics, so either type of pressure works if used consistently.\nThe cancellation in R is exactly why A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Water (density $1000$ kg m$^{-3}$) flows steadily through a horizontal pipe. At section 1 the speed is $2$ m/s and the pressure is $1.20 \\times 10^5$ Pa. At a narrower section 2 the speed is $4$ m/s. Treat water as an ideal fluid. What is the pressure at section 2?","image":"","options":["$1.14 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$1.26 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$1.18 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$1.08 \\times 10^5$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P_1 = 1.20\\times10^5$ Pa, $v_1 = 2$ m/s, $v_2 = 4$ m/s, $h_1 = h_2$.\nFormula: $P_1 - P_2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho(v_2^2 - v_1^2)$.\nSubstituting: $P_1 - P_2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\times1000\\times(16 - 4) = 6000$ Pa.\nSo $P_2 = 1.20\\times10^5 - 0.06\\times10^5 = 1.14\\times10^5$ Pa. Pressure falls where the speed rises.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the molecular picture of a liquid surface.\nStatement I: A molecule at the free surface of a liquid has a higher (less negative) potential energy than a molecule deep inside the liquid.\nStatement II: The energy needed to bring a molecule from the interior to the surface is roughly equal to the full energy needed to remove it completely from the liquid (its full share of the heat of evaporation).","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A surface molecule has attracting neighbours on only one side, roughly the lower half. So its negative potential energy is about half that of a bulk molecule, which means it has extra energy.\nStatement II is incorrect. The energy needed to place a molecule at the surface is roughly half the energy needed to remove it entirely, i.e. about half the heat of evaporation, not all of it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In the plate-balance method, a thin flat vertical glass plate with a horizontal lower edge of length 10 cm hangs from one arm of a balance. The liquid surface just touches the edge. An extra mass of 1.46 g must be added to the other pan so that the plate just clears the liquid. Neglecting the plate thickness, what is the surface tension of the liquid? (g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["0.073 N/m","0.146 N/m","0.0365 N/m","0.73 N/m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 1.46 g = $1.46 \\times 10^{-3}$ kg, l = 0.10 m.\nFormula: $S_{la} = \\frac{mg}{2l}$. The liquid pulls along both faces of the plate, so the total length in contact is 2l.\nSubstituting: $S_{la} = \\frac{1.46 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 10}{2 \\times 0.10} = \\frac{0.0146}{0.20}$.\nSo $S_{la}$ = 0.073 N/m.\nUsing l instead of 2l gives 0.146 N/m, which is the most common error.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A clean glass capillary tube of internal radius 0.20 mm is dipped vertically in water. The surface tension of water is 0.072 N/m, the angle of contact is $0^\\circ$, the density of water is 1000 kg/m$^3$ and g = 10 m/s$^2$. How high does the water rise in the tube?","image":"","options":["3.6 cm","7.2 cm","14.4 cm","0.72 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: a = $2 \\times 10^{-4}$ m, S = 0.072 N/m, $\\theta = 0$, $\\rho$ = 1000 kg/m$^3$.\nFormula: $h = \\frac{2S\\cos\\theta}{\\rho g a}$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{2 \\times 0.072 \\times 1}{1000 \\times 10 \\times 2 \\times 10^{-4}}$ = 0.072 m.\nSo h = 7.2 cm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A ball moves horizontally through still air and spins at the same time. Relative to the ball, the air flowing past its upper surface moves faster than the air flowing past its lower surface. Which of the following correctly describes the situation?","image":"","options":["Streamlines are crowded above the ball, the pressure above is lower than below, and the ball gets a net upward force","Streamlines are crowded above the ball, the pressure above is higher than below, and the ball gets a net downward force","Streamlines are symmetric above and below, so there is no vertical force on the ball","Streamlines are crowded below the ball, the pressure below is lower, and the ball gets a net downward force"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Faster air flow means closely spaced (crowded) streamlines. Here the air is faster above the ball, so streamlines crowd above it and spread out (rarify) below it.\nBy Bernoulli's principle, a higher speed means a lower pressure. So the pressure above the ball is lower than the pressure below.\nThe pressure difference gives a net upward force. This dynamic lift due to spin is called the Magnus effect.\nSymmetric streamlines (option 3) occur only for a ball that is not spinning.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Two solid spheres of the same material have radii $a$ and $2a$. Each falls through the same viscous liquid, which is less dense than the material, and reaches terminal velocity.\nStatement I: The terminal velocity of the larger sphere is 4 times that of the smaller sphere.\nStatement II: At terminal velocity, the viscous drag on the larger sphere is 8 times that on the smaller sphere.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $v_t = \\frac{2a^2(\\rho - \\sigma)g}{9\\eta}$, so $v_t \\propto a^2$. Doubling the radius gives 4 times the terminal velocity.\nStatement II is correct. The drag is $F = 6\\pi\\eta a v_t \\propto a \\cdot a^2 = a^3$, giving a ratio of $2^3 = 8$.\nCheck: at terminal velocity, drag = weight − buoyancy = $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi a^3(\\rho - \\sigma)g$, which also scales as $a^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following instruments read gauge pressure (pressure relative to atmospheric) rather than absolute pressure?\n(i) Tyre pressure gauge\n(ii) Sphygmomanometer (blood pressure gauge)\n(iii) Open-tube manometer\n(iv) Mercury barometer","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Gauge pressure: NCERT lists the tyre pressure gauge as a gauge-pressure device.\n(ii) Gauge pressure: the sphygmomanometer is also listed.\n(iii) Gauge pressure: the open end of the manometer is at atmospheric pressure, so its height h gives $P - P_a$.\n(iv) Absolute: the space above the mercury is at about zero pressure, so the barometer reads the full atmospheric pressure.\nThat makes 3 gauge-pressure devices.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a liquid escaping through a small hole in a tank where the gas above the liquid is at a very high pressure, as in rocket propulsion, the speed of efflux depends mainly on the pressure in the container and hardly on the height of the liquid above the hole.\nReason (R): The speed of efflux is $v = \\sqrt{2gh + \\frac{2(P - P_a)}{\\rho}}$, and when $P \\gg P_a$ the pressure term is much larger than $2gh$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Applying Bernoulli's equation between the liquid surface (at pressure P) and the hole (at pressure $P_a$) gives $v^2 = 2gh + \\frac{2(P - P_a)}{\\rho}$. So R states a correct result.\nWhen $P \\gg P_a$, the term $\\frac{2(P - P_a)}{\\rho}$ dominates and $2gh$ can be ignored. The efflux speed is then set by the container pressure, which is what A states.\nNCERT cites rocket propulsion as exactly this case. So A is true and R explains it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"The coefficient of viscosity $\\eta$ is defined as the ratio of shearing stress to strain rate. What are its dimensions?","image":"","options":["$[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$","$[ML^{-1}T^{-1}]$","$[MLT^{-1}]$","$[ML^{-2}T^{-1}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Shearing stress = force/area, with dimensions $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$.\nStrain rate = $\\frac{v}{l}$, with dimensions $[T^{-1}]$.\nSo $\\eta = \\frac{[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]}{[T^{-1}]} = [ML^{-1}T^{-1}]$.\nOption (1) gives the dimensions of pressure or stress, not viscosity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In the NCERT proof of Pascal's law, a fluid element in the form of a small right-angled prism is considered inside a fluid at rest. Why can the effect of gravity on this element be ignored?","image":"","options":["The fluid is assumed to be incompressible, so its weight is zero","The element is so small that every part of it is effectively at the same depth","The normal forces on the faces are much larger than atmospheric pressure","Gravity acts only on the walls of the container, not on interior fluid elements"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT takes the prismatic element to be very small.\nThis means every part of it is effectively at the same depth below the liquid surface, so gravity affects all its points equally. Its weight can then be neglected compared with the surface forces.\nOption (1) fails because incompressibility makes the density constant, not the weight zero.\nOption (4) is false because gravity acts on every fluid element.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"The pistons of a hydraulic press have diameters of 2 cm and 10 cm. What is the mechanical advantage of the press?","image":"","options":["5","10","25","125"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $d_1$ = 2 cm, $d_2$ = 10 cm.\nFormula: mechanical advantage = $\\frac{A_2}{A_1} = \\left(\\frac{d_2}{d_1}\\right)^2$, because area is proportional to the square of the diameter.\nSubstituting: $(10/2)^2 = 5^2$.\nSo the mechanical advantage = 25. The tempting answer 5 is only the ratio of diameters, not of areas.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A liquid rests on a flat solid surface in air. The interfacial tensions are: liquid–air $S_{la} = 0.070$ N/m, solid–air $S_{sa} = 0.100$ N/m and solid–liquid $S_{sl} = 0.065$ N/m. Using the equilibrium condition at the line of contact, find the angle of contact.","image":"","options":["30°","90°","60°","120°"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $S_{la} = 0.070$, $S_{sa} = 0.100$, $S_{sl} = 0.065$ (all in N/m).\nFormula: $S_{la}\\cos\\theta + S_{sl} = S_{sa}$, which gives $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{S_{sa} - S_{sl}}{S_{la}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{0.100 - 0.065}{0.070} = 0.5$.\nSo θ = 60°, an acute angle, and the liquid wets the surface.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In an open-tube manometer, a low-density liquid such as oil is preferred for measuring small pressure differences.\nReason (R): For a given gauge pressure, the difference in liquid levels in the two arms of the manometer is inversely proportional to the density of the manometric liquid.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that oil is used for small pressure differences and mercury for large ones.\nR is true: the gauge pressure is $P - P_a = \\rho g h$, so $h = \\frac{P - P_a}{\\rho g}$. A smaller $\\rho$ therefore gives a larger h.\nR explains A: a small pressure difference gives a level difference large enough to read only when $\\rho$ is small.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In a hydraulic lift, a force of 200 N is applied to the small piston of area 10 $cm^2$. The large piston has an area of 500 $cm^2$. What upward force acts on the large piston? (Ignore the weights of the pistons and any height difference.)","image":"","options":["4 N","4000 N","10000 N","100000 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $F_1$ = 200 N, $A_1$ = 10 $cm^2$, $A_2$ = 500 $cm^2$.\nFormula: pressure is transmitted undiminished, so $\\frac{F_1}{A_1} = \\frac{F_2}{A_2}$, which gives $F_2 = F_1\\frac{A_2}{A_1}$.\nSubstituting: $F_2 = 200 \\times \\frac{500}{10} = 200 \\times 50$.\nSo $F_2$ = 10000 N. The area ratio can be used directly because the units cancel.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A metal block of base area 0.040 m$^2$ rests on a liquid film of thickness 0.40 mm spread on a horizontal table. A string attached to the block passes over an ideal pulley and carries a hanging mass of 0.012 kg. On release, the block moves with a constant speed of 0.15 m/s.\nThe same block is now placed on a film of the same liquid (same temperature) of thickness 0.60 mm, and the hanging mass is replaced by 0.030 kg. Assuming a linear velocity profile across the film, what is the new constant speed of the block? (Take g = 10 m/s$^2$.)","image":"","options":["0.25 m/s","0.375 m/s","0.5625 m/s","0.09 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: at constant speed the tension equals the weight of the hanging mass, so the viscous force is $F = mg$.\nFormula: $\\eta = \\frac{F/A}{v/l}$, which gives $v = \\frac{mgl}{\\eta A}$.\nFirst case: $\\eta = \\frac{0.12 \\times 0.40\\times10^{-3}}{0.040 \\times 0.15} = 8\\times10^{-3}$ Pa s.\nSubstituting for the second case: $v = \\frac{0.30 \\times 0.60\\times10^{-3}}{8\\times10^{-3}\\times0.040} = 0.5625$ m/s.\nCheck by ratios: $0.15 \\times \\frac{30}{12} \\times \\frac{0.60}{0.40}$ = 0.5625 m/s. A thicker film lowers the velocity gradient, so the speed increases rather than decreases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following work directly on Pascal's law, that is, on the transmission of pressure undiminished through an enclosed fluid?\n(i) Hydraulic lift for cars\n(ii) Hydraulic brakes of an automobile\n(iii) Upward lift on an aeroplane wing\n(iv) Swing of a spinning cricket ball\n(v) Speed of liquid flowing out of a hole in an open tank","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: the pressure $F_1/A_1$ is transmitted to the larger piston, giving $F_2 = F_1A_2/A_1$.\n(ii) Yes: the pressure from the pedal reaches all four wheel cylinders through the brake oil.\n(iii) No: wing lift is dynamic lift, explained by Bernoulli's principle.\n(iv) No: the swing of a spinning ball is the Magnus effect, also explained by Bernoulli's principle.\n(v) No: the speed of efflux follows Torricelli's law, which comes from Bernoulli's equation.\nSo 2 of them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In level flight, the air speed over the upper surface of a small aircraft's wings is 60 m/s and under the lower surface is 50 m/s. The total wing area is 20 m$^2$ and the density of air is 1.2 kg/m$^3$. Neglecting the small height difference between the two surfaces, what is the dynamic lift on the wings?","image":"","options":["$6.6 \\times 10^3$ N","$1.2 \\times 10^4$ N","$1.32 \\times 10^4$ N","$2.64 \\times 10^4$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $v_2$ = 60 m/s (top), $v_1$ = 50 m/s (bottom), A = 20 m$^2$, $\\rho$ = 1.2 kg/m$^3$.\nFormula: $\\Delta P = \\frac{\\rho}{2}(v_2^2 - v_1^2)$ and Lift = $\\Delta P \\times A$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta P = 0.6 \\times (3600 - 2500) = 660$ Pa.\nLift = 660 $\\times$ 20 = 13200 N = $1.32 \\times 10^4$ N. Option (1) comes from omitting the factor of 2 in the wing area or the density product.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A small steel ball of radius $r$ falls through glycerine with a terminal velocity of 2 cm/s. Another steel ball of radius $2r$ is dropped in the same glycerine. What is its terminal velocity?","image":"","options":["8 cm/s","4 cm/s","16 cm/s","2 cm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $v_1 = 2$ cm/s, $a_2 = 2a_1$. The material and the liquid are the same.\nFormula: $v_t = \\frac{2a^2(\\rho-\\sigma)g}{9\\eta}$, so $v_t \\propto a^2$.\nSubstituting: $v_2 = v_1 \\times (2)^2 = 2 \\times 4$.\nSo $v_2 = 8$ cm/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Each of the three terms in Bernoulli's equation $P + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2 + \\rho g h = \\text{constant}$ has the same dimensions. These dimensions are:","image":"","options":["$[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$","$[ML^{2}T^{-2}]$","$[ML^{-3}]$","$[MLT^{-2}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Pressure P has dimensions $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$ (force per unit area).\n$\\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2$ gives $[ML^{-3}][L^2T^{-2}] = [ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$, which is kinetic energy per unit volume.\n$\\rho g h$ gives $[ML^{-3}][LT^{-2}][L] = [ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$, which is potential energy per unit volume.\nEnergy per unit volume has the same dimensions as pressure. $[ML^2T^{-2}]$ is energy itself, not energy per unit volume.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Water flows steadily through a horizontal pipe. At section 1 the area of cross-section is $4\\ \\text{cm}^2$ and the speed is $2\\ \\text{m/s}$. At section 2 the area is $1\\ \\text{cm}^2$. Treat water as incompressible. What is the speed of water at section 2?","image":"","options":["8 m/s","0.5 m/s","2 m/s","16 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A_1 = 4\\ \\text{cm}^2$, $v_1 = 2$ m/s, $A_2 = 1\\ \\text{cm}^2$.\nFormula: equation of continuity, $A_1v_1 = A_2v_2$.\nSubstituting: $v_2 = \\frac{4 \\times 2}{1}$.\nSo $v_2 = 8$ m/s. The fluid speeds up where the pipe is narrower.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A diver is 5 m below the surface of a freshwater lake. What is the gauge pressure at her position? (Density of water = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, g = 10 m s$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$5.0 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa","$1.5 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa","$5.0 \\times 10^{3}$ Pa","$1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 5 m, $\\rho$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, g = 10 m s$^{-2}$.\nFormula: gauge pressure $P - P_a = \\rho g h$.\nSubstituting: $1000 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 5.0 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa.\n$1.5 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa would be the absolute pressure, which also includes atmospheric pressure.\nSo the gauge pressure = $5.0 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A large tank open to the atmosphere is filled with water. A small hole is made in its side wall at a point 5 m below the free surface of the water. Take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$. What is the speed with which water first flows out of the hole?","image":"","options":["10 m/s","5 m/s","14.1 m/s","50 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the tank is open, so $P = P_a$. The depth of the hole below the surface is h = 5 m and g = 10 m/s².\nFormula: Torricelli's law gives $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$. The tank is wide compared with the hole, so the speed of the top surface is taken as zero.\nSubstituting: $v = \\sqrt{2 \\times 10 \\times 5} = \\sqrt{100}$.\nSo v = 10 m/s. This is the same speed a body gains by falling freely through 5 m. The value 50 comes from forgetting the square root.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A closed tank contains water (density $1000$ kg/m$^3$). The air above the water is at a pressure $3.2 \\times 10^4$ Pa above atmospheric pressure. A small hole in the side of the tank is 1.8 m below the water surface, and the tank is much wider than the hole. Find the speed of efflux. (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["6 m/s","8 m/s","10 m/s","12 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $P - P_a = 3.2 \\times 10^4$ Pa, h = 1.8 m, $\\rho = 1000$ kg/m$^3$.\nFormula: $v = \\sqrt{2gh + \\frac{2(P-P_a)}{\\rho}}$.\nSubstituting: 2gh = 36 m$^2$/s$^2$ and $\\frac{2 \\times 3.2\\times10^4}{1000} = 64$ m$^2$/s$^2$.\nSo $v = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100}$ = 10 m/s. Ignoring the excess pressure gives 6 m/s; ignoring the depth gives 8 m/s.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Torricelli's result $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$ gives the speed of efflux from a hole at depth h below the liquid surface. How many of the following conditions are assumed in obtaining this result?\n(i) The top of the tank is open to the atmosphere.\n(ii) The tank's cross-sectional area is much larger than that of the hole.\n(iii) The liquid is non-viscous and incompressible, and the flow is steady.\n(iv) The liquid must be water.\n(v) The hole must be at the bottom of the tank.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Assumed: with P = $P_a$, the pressure term $\\frac{2(P-P_a)}{\\rho}$ drops out.\n(ii) Assumed: $A_2 \\gg A_1$ lets us take $v_2 \\approx 0$ at the top surface.\n(iii) Assumed: Bernoulli's equation holds only for steady flow of non-viscous, incompressible fluids.\n(iv) Not needed: the density cancels, so the result holds for any such liquid.\n(v) Not needed: h is the depth of the hole below the surface, wherever the hole is.\nThree conditions are assumed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For the same radius and the same surface tension, the excess pressure inside a soap bubble in air is twice the excess pressure inside a liquid drop.\nStatement II: The excess pressure inside an air bubble of radius r formed within a liquid of surface tension S is $\\frac{4S}{r}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. A soap bubble in air has two liquid-air interfaces, so its excess pressure is $\\frac{4S}{r}$. A drop has one interface, so its excess pressure is $\\frac{2S}{r}$.\nStatement II is false. An air bubble inside a liquid (a cavity) has only one liquid surface, so its excess pressure is $\\frac{2S}{r}$, not $\\frac{4S}{r}$.\nNCERT stresses this distinction in Example 9.10.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In a hydraulic lift, a force $F_1$ acts on the small piston of area $A_1$, which moves in through a distance $L_1$. The large piston has area $A_2$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Pressure in the liquid\n(B) Force on the large piston\n(C) Mechanical advantage\n(D) Distance moved by the large piston\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{A_2}{A_1}$\n(ii) $\\frac{L_1A_1}{A_2}$\n(iii) $\\frac{F_1}{A_1}$\n(iv) $\\frac{F_1A_2}{A_1}$","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The pressure set up in the liquid is $P = F_1/A_1$, so A matches (iii).\n(B) The same pressure acts on $A_2$, giving $F_2 = PA_2 = F_1A_2/A_1$, so B matches (iv).\n(C) The force is increased by the factor $A_2/A_1$, which is the mechanical advantage, so C matches (i).\n(D) Equal volumes give $L_1A_1 = L_2A_2$, so $L_2 = L_1A_1/A_2$ and D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Match each fluid in List-I with its approximate viscosity in List-II (values from NCERT Table 9.2).\nList-I\n(A) Water at 20 °C\n(B) Blood at 37 °C\n(C) Glycerine at 20 °C\n(D) Air at 0 °C\nList-II\n(I) 0.017 mPl\n(II) 830 mPl\n(III) 2.7 mPl\n(IV) 1.0 mPl","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Water at 20 °C has a viscosity of 1.0 mPl, so A-IV.\nBlood is 'thicker' than water, at 2.7 mPl, so B-III.\nGlycerine is highly viscous, at 830 mPl, so C-II.\nAir has a very low viscosity, 0.017 mPl, so D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A diver is 5.0 m below the surface of a freshwater lake (density $1000\\ kg\\ m^{-3}$). What is the gauge pressure on the diver? (Take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$, atmospheric pressure $= 1.01 \\times 10^5$ Pa.)","image":"","options":["$5.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa","$1.51 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$5.0 \\times 10^3$ Pa","$1.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 5.0 m, $\\rho = 1000\\ kg\\ m^{-3}$, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: gauge pressure $P_g = P - P_a = \\rho g h$.\nSubstituting: $P_g = 1000 \\times 10 \\times 5.0 = 5.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa.\n$1.51 \\times 10^5$ Pa is the absolute pressure, not the gauge pressure.\nSo gauge pressure = $5.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A force of 80 N is applied uniformly on a flat surface of area 25 $cm^2$. The force is directed at 30° to the plane of the surface (not to its normal). What is the average pressure on the surface?","image":"","options":["$1.6 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa","$2.77 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa","$3.2 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa","$0.8 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: F = 80 N at 30° to the plane, A = $25 \\times 10^{-4}$ $m^2$.\nOnly the normal component of the force produces pressure. At 30° to the plane, this component is F sin 30° = 40 N.\nFormula: P = $F_{normal}$/A.\nSubstituting: P = 40 / ($25 \\times 10^{-4}$) = $1.6 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa.\nThe option $2.77 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa uses the parallel component (F cos 30°), and $3.2 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa uses the full force. Both are wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Consider an ideal hydraulic lift with an incompressible liquid and frictionless pistons at the same level. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The increase in pressure at the large piston equals the increase in pressure at the small piston.\n(ii) The volume of liquid pushed in by the small piston equals the volume that moves up under the large piston.\n(iii) The work done by the applied force on the small piston equals the work done by the large piston on the load.\n(iv) The large piston moves through a greater distance than the small piston.\n(v) Atmospheric pressure acts on both pistons and can be ignored when relating $F_1$ and $F_2$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: pressure is transmitted undiminished through the liquid.\n(ii) Correct: the liquid is incompressible, so $L_1A_1 = L_2A_2$.\n(iii) Correct: $F_1L_1 = \\frac{F_1}{A_1} \\times L_1A_1 = \\frac{F_2}{A_2} \\times L_2A_2 = F_2L_2$, so energy is conserved in the ideal case.\n(iv) Incorrect: $L_2 = L_1A_1/A_2 < L_1$, so the large piston moves less.\n(v) Correct: atmospheric pressure is common to both pistons, as NCERT notes in the syringe example.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"The upper part of a person's body, of mass 45 kg, is supported by the two thigh bones (femurs). Each femur has a cross-sectional area of 9 $cm^2$. What is the average pressure sustained by the femurs? (g = 10 m $s^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$2.5 \\times 10^{3}$ Pa","$2.5 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa","$5.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa","$2.5 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: m = 45 kg, so F = mg = 450 N. Total area A = 2 × 9 = 18 $cm^2$ = $18 \\times 10^{-4}$ $m^2$.\nFormula: $P_{av} = \\frac{F}{A}$. The weight acts vertically, so it is normal to the bone cross-section.\nSubstituting: $P = \\frac{450}{18 \\times 10^{-4}}$.\nSo P = $2.5 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.\nUsing only one femur gives $5 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Eight identical spherical raindrops each fall through still air with a terminal velocity of 4.0 cm/s. They merge to form one larger spherical drop. Assume the densities of water and air, and the viscosity of air, are unchanged. What is the terminal velocity of the larger drop?","image":"","options":["16 cm/s","8 cm/s","32 cm/s","64 cm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 8 drops of radius a, each with $v_t = 4.0$ cm/s.\nVolume is conserved: $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3 = 8 \\times \\frac{4}{3}\\pi a^3$, so R = 2a.\nFormula (Stokes' law): $v_t = \\frac{2a^2(\\rho - \\sigma)g}{9\\eta}$, so $v_t \\propto a^2$.\nSubstituting: the new $v_t = 4.0 \\times (2)^2 = 16$ cm/s.\n8 cm/s wrongly assumes $v_t \\propto a$. 32 cm/s assumes $v_t$ scales with volume.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"The lower end of a vertical capillary tube of internal radius 0.50 mm is dipped 5.0 cm below the surface of water. Air is blown into the tube from the top to form a hemispherical bubble at its lower end. The bubble's radius equals the tube's radius. By how much must the air pressure in the tube exceed atmospheric pressure? (Surface tension of water = 0.072 N/m, density of water = 1000 kg/m$^3$, g = 10 m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["288 Pa","644 Pa","1076 Pa","788 Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: r = $5.0\\times10^{-4}$ m, depth = 0.05 m, S = 0.072 N/m.\nPressure just outside the bubble = $P_a + \\rho g h$, with $\\rho g h = 1000\\times10\\times0.05 = 500$ Pa.\nAn air bubble inside a liquid has only one surface, so the excess pressure is $\\frac{2S}{r} = \\frac{2\\times0.072}{5\\times10^{-4}} = 288$ Pa. (Using 4S/r would wrongly give 1076 Pa.)\nSo $P_i - P_a = 500 + 288 = 788$ Pa.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Water flows steadily out of a vertical tap whose mouth has a cross-sectional area of $1.0\\ \\text{cm}^2$. The water leaves the mouth with a speed of 1.0 m/s. The falling stream stays continuous and the water is incompressible; neglect air resistance and take $g = 10\\ \\text{m/s}^2$. What is the cross-sectional area of the stream 15 cm below the mouth?","image":"","options":["0.50 $\\text{cm}^2$","0.25 $\\text{cm}^2$","0.71 $\\text{cm}^2$","2.0 $\\text{cm}^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A_1 = 1.0\\ \\text{cm}^2$, $v_1 = 1.0$ m/s, h = 0.15 m.\nFormula: free fall gives $v_2^2 = v_1^2 + 2gh$, and continuity gives $A_1v_1 = A_2v_2$.\nSubstituting: $v_2^2 = 1 + 2 \\times 10 \\times 0.15 = 4$, so $v_2 = 2$ m/s. Then $A_2 = \\frac{1.0 \\times 1}{2}$.\nSo $A_2 = 0.50\\ \\text{cm}^2$. The stream narrows because the water speeds up as it falls.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"The force exerted by a fluid at rest on any surface in contact with it is always perpendicular to that surface. The reason is that:","image":"","options":["a parallel component would, by Newton's third law, make the surface push the fluid along itself, so the fluid would flow, contradicting the fluid being at rest","the fluid molecules are always attracted perpendicular to a solid surface because of surface tension","gravity acts vertically, so every fluid force must also be vertical","the density of a fluid at rest is the same at all points"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"If the fluid's force had a component parallel to the surface, the surface would exert an equal and opposite parallel force on the fluid (Newton's third law).\nFluids offer almost no resistance to shear, so such a force would make the fluid flow along the surface.\nA fluid at rest does not flow, so the parallel component must be zero.\nOption (3) fails because the force is normal even on vertical or slanted walls.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Consider an ideal hydraulic lift with a small piston of area $A_1$ and a large piston of area $A_2$, filled with an incompressible liquid.\nStatement I: Since the mechanical advantage of the lift is $\\frac{A_2}{A_1}$, the work output at the large piston is $\\frac{A_2}{A_1}$ times the work input at the small piston.\nStatement II: If the small piston moves in through a distance $L_1$, the large piston moves out through a distance $L_2$ such that $L_1 A_1 = L_2 A_2$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. The liquid is incompressible, so the volume pushed out by the small piston equals the volume received by the large piston: $L_1 A_1 = L_2 A_2$.\nStatement I is incorrect. The force is multiplied, $F_2 = F_1 \\frac{A_2}{A_1}$, but the distance moved is reduced, $L_2 = L_1 \\frac{A_1}{A_2}$.\nWork: $F_2 L_2 = F_1 L_1$. An ideal hydraulic lift multiplies force, not energy.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a real (viscous) liquid flows steadily through a long horizontal pipe of uniform cross-section, the pressure decreases along the direction of flow.\nReason (R): Bernoulli's equation, applied between two points of a horizontal pipe of uniform cross-section, predicts that the pressures at the two points are equal.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Viscous forces between the fluid layers do dissipative work and turn kinetic energy into heat. NCERT notes that the downstream pressure is then lower than the value Bernoulli's equation gives. In a uniform pipe the speed cannot fall (continuity), so the pressure must drop.\nR is true. With equal $v$ (continuity) and equal $h$, $P + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2 + \\rho g h$ = constant gives $P_1 = P_2$.\nR is not the explanation. It predicts no pressure drop; the drop in A comes from viscosity, which Bernoulli's equation ignores.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A copper ball (density $8.9 \\times 10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$) is released from rest in a deep tank of oil (density $1.5 \\times 10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$). Stokes' law holds throughout the motion. Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$. What is the acceleration of the ball at the instant its speed equals half of its terminal velocity?","image":"","options":["4.16 m/s$^2$","5.00 m/s$^2$","8.31 m/s$^2$","2.08 m/s$^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\rho = 8.9\\times10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$, $\\sigma = 1.5\\times10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$, and $v = \\frac{v_t}{2}$.\nThe net force is $V(\\rho-\\sigma)g - 6\\pi\\eta a v$. At $v = v_t$, the drag equals $V(\\rho-\\sigma)g$, and drag is proportional to $v$.\nSo at $v = \\frac{v_t}{2}$, the net force is $\\frac{1}{2}V(\\rho-\\sigma)g$.\nThe acceleration is $\\frac{(\\rho-\\sigma)g}{2\\rho} = \\frac{7.4 \\times 10}{2 \\times 8.9}$.\nSo the acceleration is $\\approx 4.16$ m/s$^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A U-tube holds mercury at the bottom. Water is poured into one arm and an immiscible oil into the other. When the mercury levels in both arms are equal, the water column is 12.0 cm high and the oil column is 15.0 cm high, both measured above the mercury. If the density of water is 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, what is the density of the oil?","image":"","options":["800 kg m$^{-3}$","1250 kg m$^{-3}$","750 kg m$^{-3}$","900 kg m$^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $h_w$ = 12.0 cm, $h_o$ = 15.0 cm, $\\rho_w$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$.\nFormula: the mercury surfaces are at the same level, so the pressures there are equal: $\\rho_w g h_w = \\rho_o g h_o$.\nSubstituting: $\\rho_o = \\frac{1000 \\times 12}{15} = 800$ kg m$^{-3}$.\nSo the oil density = 800 kg m$^{-3}$. Inverting the ratio gives 1250, which would make the taller column the denser liquid, which is impossible.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: At any point inside a fluid at rest, the pressure has the same value whatever the orientation of the small area considered.\nStatement II: Like a liquid, a gas has a nearly constant density over a wide range of pressures.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The prism-element proof gives $P_a = P_b = P_c$, so pressure at a point is the same in all directions.\nStatement II is incorrect. A liquid is largely incompressible, so its density is nearly constant. Gases, however, show a large variation in density with pressure.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Water (density $1000$ kg m$^{-3}$) flows steadily through a horizontal pipe. At point X its speed is 2 m/s and at point Y its speed is 4 m/s. Treating water as ideal, the pressure at X exceeds the pressure at Y by:","image":"","options":["2000 Pa","6000 Pa","8000 Pa","12000 Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\rho = 1000$ kg m$^{-3}$, $v_X = 2$ m/s, $v_Y = 4$ m/s, horizontal pipe so $h_X = h_Y$.\nFormula: $P_X - P_Y = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho(v_Y^2 - v_X^2)$\nSubstituting: $P_X - P_Y = 0.5 \\times 1000 \\times (16 - 4)$\nSo $P_X - P_Y = 6000$ Pa.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A small spherical mercury drop of radius 1.5 mm is in air. The surface tension of mercury is 0.48 N/m. What is the excess pressure inside the drop?","image":"","options":["320 Pa","640 Pa","1280 Pa","160 Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: r = $1.5 \\times 10^{-3}$ m, S = 0.48 N/m.\nFormula: a liquid drop has a single surface, so $P_i - P_o = \\frac{2S}{r}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{2 \\times 0.48}{1.5 \\times 10^{-3}}$ = 640 Pa.\nUsing $\\frac{4S}{r}$ would wrongly give 1280 Pa.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A drop in the barometric mercury level by 10 mm or more is a sign of an approaching storm","A pressure of 1 mm of Hg is called one torr","For measuring small pressure differences, an open-tube manometer should use a high-density liquid such as mercury","If water replaced mercury in a barometer, the column at sea level would be roughly 10 m tall"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT notes that a drop of 10 mm or more in the Hg level signals a storm.\n(2) Correct: 1 torr = 1 mm of Hg.\n(3) Incorrect: a low-density liquid such as oil gives a larger h for a small $\\Delta P$, since $h = \\frac{\\Delta P}{\\rho g}$. Mercury suits large pressure differences.\n(4) Correct: $h_w = 0.76 \\times 13.6 \\approx 10.3$ m.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A diver is 5.0 m below the surface of a freshwater lake. Taking the density of water as $1000$ kg m$^{-3}$, $g = 10$ m/s$^2$ and atmospheric pressure as $1.01 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, what is the gauge pressure on the diver?","image":"","options":["$5.0 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa","$1.51 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa","$5.0 \\times 10^{3}$ Pa","$1.01 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 5.0 m, $\\rho$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: gauge pressure $P_g = P - P_a = \\rho g h$.\nSubstituting: $P_g = 1000 \\times 10 \\times 5.0 = 5.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa.\nThe value $1.51 \\times 10^5$ Pa is the absolute pressure, not the gauge pressure.\nSo the gauge pressure is $5.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Steady flow is achieved at low flow speeds.\n(ii) Beyond a limiting value called the critical speed, the flow loses steadiness and becomes turbulent.\n(iii) In laminar flow, the velocities at all points in the fluid must have the same magnitude.\n(iv) The foamy, whirlpool-like 'white water rapids' formed when a fast stream meets rocks are an example of turbulent flow.\n(v) In steady flow, the velocity of a given fluid particle cannot change as it moves from one point to another.","image":"","options":["3","2","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT says steady flow occurs at low speeds.\n(ii) Correct: above the critical speed the flow becomes turbulent.\n(iii) Incorrect: in laminar flow the speeds at different points may differ, though the directions are parallel.\n(iv) Correct: rapids are NCERT's example of turbulence.\n(v) Incorrect: a particle may change velocity from point to point. Only the velocity at a fixed point stays constant in time.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"An open tank contains a 0.5 m layer of oil (density 800 kg m$^{-3}$) floating on a 1.0 m deep layer of water (density 1000 kg m$^{-3}$). The liquids do not mix. What is the gauge pressure at the bottom of the tank? (g = 10 m s$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["$1.4 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa","$1.5 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa","$9.0 \\times 10^{3}$ Pa","$1.8 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: oil layer 0.5 m with $\\rho_1$ = 800 kg m$^{-3}$; water layer 1.0 m with $\\rho_2$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$.\nFormula: apply $P_2 - P_1 = \\rho g h$ to each layer and add: $P_g = \\rho_1 g h_1 + \\rho_2 g h_2$.\nSubstituting: $800 \\times 10 \\times 0.5 + 1000 \\times 10 \\times 1.0 = 4000 + 10000$.\nSo the gauge pressure = $1.4 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa. Taking the full 1.5 m as water would wrongly give $1.5 \\times 10^{4}$ Pa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are conditions for Bernoulli's equation $P + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2 + \\rho g h = \\text{constant}$ to hold strictly, as discussed in NCERT?\n(i) The fluid has zero (negligible) viscosity\n(ii) The fluid is incompressible\n(iii) The flow is steady\n(iv) The flow is not turbulent\n(v) The pipe must be horizontal\n(vi) The pipe must have a uniform cross-section","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Required: viscosity dissipates energy as heat, so mechanical energy is not conserved.\n(ii) Required: the elastic energy of a compressible fluid is ignored in the derivation.\n(iii) and (iv) Required: in unsteady or turbulent flow, velocity and pressure fluctuate in time.\n(v) Not required: the $\\rho g h$ term handles height changes.\n(vi) Not required: the derivation uses a pipe of varying area.\nSo 4 conditions are needed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Suppose the density of air were a uniform 1.25 kg m$^{-3}$ at all heights and the sea-level atmospheric pressure were $1.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa. Taking g = 10 m/s$^2$ (constant), how high would the atmosphere extend?","image":"","options":["8.0 km","80 km","0.8 km","16 km"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\rho$ = 1.25 kg m$^{-3}$, $P_a = 1.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: the whole air column produces $P_a$, so $\\rho g h = P_a$ and $h = \\frac{P_a}{\\rho g}$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{10^5}{1.25 \\times 10} = 8000$ m.\nSo h = 8.0 km. In reality air density and g both fall with height, so the atmosphere extends beyond 100 km.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A mercury barometer reads 76.0 cm in a stationary lift. The lift then accelerates upward at 2.5 m/s$^2$, and the air pressure inside the lift stays the same. Taking g = 10 m/s$^2$, what is the new height of the mercury column?","image":"","options":["60.8 cm","95.0 cm","76.0 cm","57.0 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $h_0$ = 76.0 cm at rest, a = 2.5 m/s$^2$ upward, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nIdea: in the lift frame the effective gravity is $g + a$, so the column now satisfies $P_a = \\rho (g+a) h$.\nAt rest: $P_a = \\rho g h_0$. Equating the two gives $h = \\frac{h_0 g}{g+a}$.\nSubstituting: $h = \\frac{76 \\times 10}{12.5} = 60.8$ cm.\nThe value 95.0 cm comes from inverting the ratio. The value 57.0 cm uses $g - a$, which would apply to a downward acceleration.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A wide open tank stands on level ground with its water surface 5.0 m above the ground. Water squirts horizontally from a small hole in the side wall at a height of 1.8 m above the ground. At what horizontal distance from the wall does the jet strike the ground? (Neglect air resistance; take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["2.4 m","3.6 m","4.8 m","6.4 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: depth of hole below surface h = 5.0 − 1.8 = 3.2 m, and height of hole above ground y = 1.8 m.\nEfflux speed: $v = \\sqrt{2gh} = \\sqrt{2\\times10\\times3.2} = 8$ m/s, directed horizontally.\nTime of fall: $t = \\sqrt{\\frac{2y}{g}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{3.6}{10}} = 0.6$ s.\nRange: x = vt = 8 $\\times$ 0.6 = 4.8 m. Using the full 5.0 m as h would give a wrong value.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A swimmer is 2 m below the surface of a lake. How deep below the surface must she go for the absolute pressure on her to become exactly twice its value at 2 m? (Atmospheric pressure = $1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, density of water = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, g = 10 m s$^{-2}$)","image":"","options":["14 m","4 m","12 m","24 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P_a = 1.0 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, $\\rho g$ = $10^{4}$ Pa per m.\nAt 2 m: $P = 1.0 \\times 10^{5} + 2 \\times 10^{4} = 1.2 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.\nRequired pressure: $2.4 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa, so $\\rho g h = 2.4 \\times 10^{5} - 1.0 \\times 10^{5} = 1.4 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa.\nSo $h = \\frac{1.4 \\times 10^{5}}{10^{4}}$ = 14 m.\nDoubling the depth to 4 m only doubles the gauge part, not the absolute pressure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"All the liquids below have the same surface tension S. Match each object in List-I with the excess pressure inside it in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Liquid drop of radius r in air\n(B) Soap bubble of radius r in air\n(C) Air bubble of radius 2r inside the liquid\n(D) Soap bubble of diameter r in air\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{S}{r}$\n(II) $\\frac{2S}{r}$\n(III) $\\frac{4S}{r}$\n(IV) $\\frac{8S}{r}$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A drop has one surface: $\\frac{2S}{r}$, which is (II).\n(B) A soap bubble has two surfaces: $\\frac{4S}{r}$, which is (III).\n(C) An air bubble in a liquid has one surface: $\\frac{2S}{2r} = \\frac{S}{r}$, which is (I).\n(D) The radius is $\\frac{r}{2}$, so the excess pressure is $\\frac{4S}{r/2} = \\frac{8S}{r}$, which is (IV).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The gauge pressure at depth h in a liquid open to the atmosphere does not depend on the base area or shape of the container.\n(ii) In a connected liquid at rest, the pressure is the same at all points in the same horizontal plane.\n(iii) At a depth of 10 m in water ($\\rho$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, g = 10 m s$^{-2}$), the gauge pressure is roughly 1 atm.\n(iv) The relation $P = P_a + \\rho g h$ with constant $\\rho$ accurately describes the pressure throughout the entire height of the earth's atmosphere.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the gauge pressure $\\rho g h$ contains no area term; only the height of the liquid column matters.\n(ii) Correct: unequal pressures in a horizontal plane would cause flow, so a liquid at rest must have equal pressure there.\n(iii) Correct: $\\rho g h = 1000 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 10^{5}$ Pa, which is about 1 atm.\n(iv) Incorrect: air density, and also g, decrease with height. The constant-density estimate of about 8 km fails, and the atmosphere actually extends beyond 100 km.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the steady flow of a fluid are correct?\n(i) Two streamlines may cross each other at a point.\n(ii) The tangent to a streamline at any point gives the direction of fluid velocity at that point.\n(iii) At any fixed point, the velocity of each passing fluid particle stays constant in time.\n(iv) The velocity of the fluid is the same at all points in space.\n(v) Where streamlines are crowded together, the flow speed is greater.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Wrong: if streamlines crossed, a particle at that point could go two ways, so the flow would not be steady.\n(ii) Correct: this is the definition of a streamline.\n(iii) Correct: this is the definition of steady flow.\n(iv) Wrong: in steady flow, velocity can differ from point to point; it only stays constant in time at each point.\n(v) Correct: by $Av$ = constant, a smaller area (crowded streamlines) means a higher speed.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A horizontal cylinder fitted with a piston has three vertical open tubes attached at different points along its length. It is filled with a liquid at rest. When the piston is pushed in slowly and then held, what is observed in the three tubes?","image":"","options":["The level rises most in the tube nearest the piston","The level rises most in the tube farthest from the piston","The level rises by the same amount in all three tubes, so the levels stay equal","The levels do not change, because liquids are incompressible"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The liquid level in each tube shows the pressure at that point of the cylinder.\nPushing the piston raises the pressure, and this rise is transmitted undiminished and equally to every part of the enclosed fluid (Pascal's law).\nSo the level rises equally in all three tubes and they end at the same height.\nOption (4) fails because incompressibility does not stop pressure from being transmitted; the rise in pressure pushes liquid up the open tubes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Waterproofing agents are applied to raincoat fabric. According to NCERT, they work mainly by:","image":"","options":["lowering the surface tension of water so that it drains off quickly","making the angle of contact between water and fibres acute","increasing the viscosity of water near the fibres","creating a large angle of contact between water and the fibres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: a large (obtuse) angle of contact means the liquid does not wet the solid and forms drops instead of spreading.\nWaterproofing agents create a large angle of contact between water and the fibres, so water beads up and does not soak in.\nAn acute angle is the opposite effect. That is what wetting agents such as soaps and detergents produce, so that the liquid penetrates well.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements correctly describes a steady flow of a fluid?","image":"","options":["The velocity of the fluid is the same at every point in space","At any fixed point, the velocity of each fluid particle passing that point remains constant in time","Each fluid particle moves with the same constant velocity throughout its path","The paths of different fluid particles may cross each other"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: steady flow does not mean the velocity is the same at different points.\n(2) Correct: at a given point, every particle that passes has the same velocity at all times.\n(3) Wrong: a particle's velocity may change as it moves from one point to another.\n(4) Wrong: in steady flow the particle paths (streamlines) do not cross.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"According to Stokes' law, the viscous drag on a small sphere moving slowly through a viscous fluid is directly proportional to which power of the sphere's radius $a$?","image":"","options":["$a$","$a^2$","$a^3$","$\\frac{1}{a}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Stokes' law gives $F = 6\\pi\\eta a v$.\nThe radius appears to the first power, so $F \\propto a$ when $\\eta$ and $v$ are fixed.\nThe tempting choice $a^2$ is how the terminal velocity depends on radius, not the drag force.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In deriving Bernoulli's equation for an ideal fluid in a pipe of varying cross-section and height, the net work done by the pressure forces on a fluid element is equated to the sum of the changes in its kinetic and gravitational potential energies. Which principle is used in this step?","image":"","options":["Equation of continuity (conservation of mass)","Work–energy theorem","Conservation of linear momentum","Pascal's law of transmission of pressure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Net work by the pressure forces on the element is $(P_1 - P_2)\\Delta V$.\nThis work changes the element's kinetic energy, $\\frac{1}{2}\\rho\\Delta V(v_2^2 - v_1^2)$, and its potential energy, $\\rho g\\Delta V(h_2 - h_1)$. Equating work to the change in energy is the work–energy theorem.\nContinuity is used only to show that the same volume $\\Delta V$ passes both ends. It does not relate work to energy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A large closed tank contains water (density 1000 kg m$^{-3}$). The air above the water is at a gauge pressure of $3.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa. A small hole is in the side wall, 1.5 m below the water surface. The tank's cross-section is much larger than the hole's, and the outside is at atmospheric pressure. Find the speed of efflux of water from the hole. (Take $g = 10$ m s$^{-2}$.)","image":"","options":["5.48 m/s","7.75 m/s","9.49 m/s","13.4 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $P - P_a = 3.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa, $h = 1.5$ m, $\\rho = 1000$ kg/m³, and $v_2 \\approx 0$ at the top.\nFormula: from Bernoulli's equation, $v_1 = \\sqrt{2gh + \\frac{2(P - P_a)}{\\rho}}$.\nSubstituting: $2gh = 30$ and $\\frac{2(3 \\times 10^4)}{1000} = 60$, so $v_1 = \\sqrt{90}$.\nSo $v_1 \\approx 9.49$ m/s.\nUsing only $\\sqrt{2gh} = 5.48$ m/s ignores the pressurised air above the water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A solid aluminium cuboid (relative density 2.7) measures 20 cm × 10 cm × 5 cm. It can be placed on a horizontal table resting on any of its faces. What is the maximum average pressure it can exert on the table? (g = 10 m $s^{-2}$, density of water = $10^{3}$ kg $m^{-3}$)","image":"","options":["1350 Pa","2700 Pa","5400 Pa","540 Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\rho$ = 2.7 × $10^{3}$ kg $m^{-3}$. V = 0.2 × 0.1 × 0.05 = $10^{-3}$ $m^3$, so m = 2.7 kg and W = 27 N.\nFormula: P = W/A. Pressure is largest when the base area is smallest.\nThe smallest face is 10 cm × 5 cm = $5 \\times 10^{-3}$ $m^2$.\nSubstituting: P = 27 / ($5 \\times 10^{-3}$) = 5400 Pa. This equals $\\rho g h$ with h = 0.2 m.\n1350 Pa is the minimum pressure, on the largest face.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A diver is 5.0 m below the surface of a freshwater lake. Take atmospheric pressure $P_a = 1.01 \\times 10^5$ Pa, density of water $= 1000$ kg m$^{-3}$ and $g = 10$ m/s$^2$. What is the absolute pressure on the diver?","image":"","options":["$1.51 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$5.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa","$1.01 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$2.01 \\times 10^5$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 5.0 m, $\\rho$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, $P_a = 1.01 \\times 10^5$ Pa.\nFormula: $P = P_a + \\rho g h$.\nSubstituting: $\\rho g h = 1000 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 5.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa, so $P = 1.01 \\times 10^5 + 0.50 \\times 10^5$ Pa.\nSo P = $1.51 \\times 10^5$ Pa.\nNote that $5.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa is only the gauge pressure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A ball moving through still air without any spin experiences no upward or downward force due to the air.\nReason (R): For a non-spinning ball, the streamlines are symmetric above and below the ball, so the air speeds at corresponding points are equal and there is no pressure difference.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that the air exerts no upward or downward force on a non-spinning ball.\nR is true: the streamline pattern is symmetric, so the velocity above equals the velocity below at corresponding points.\nBy Bernoulli's principle, equal speeds mean equal pressures, giving zero net vertical force. R therefore explains A.\nA vertical force (Magnus effect) appears only when spin breaks this symmetry.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In a steady flow, a streamline is drawn through the fluid. What does the tangent drawn to the streamline at any point give?","image":"","options":["The direction of the fluid velocity at that point","The direction of the pressure gradient at that point","The direction of the fluid acceleration at that point","The direction perpendicular to the fluid velocity at that point"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT defines a streamline as the path of a fluid particle in steady flow. It is a curve whose tangent at any point is along the fluid velocity there.\nAcceleration need not point along the velocity. On a curved streamline, part of the acceleration is directed towards the centre of curvature, so option (3) fails.\nThe velocity lies along the tangent, not perpendicular to it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"On a day when the atmospheric pressure supports a vertical column of 75 cm of mercury, a 2.0 m long Torricelli barometer tube is tilted. Its axis now makes an angle of $30^\\circ$ with the horizontal. What length of the tube is now occupied by mercury, measured along the tube from the trough surface?","image":"","options":["150 cm","86.6 cm","37.5 cm","75 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: vertical height h = 75 cm, angle with horizontal = $30^\\circ$, tube length 200 cm.\nKey idea: $P_a = \\rho g h$ fixes only the vertical height of the column. Tilting cannot change h.\nGeometry: $L \\sin 30^\\circ = h$, so $L = \\frac{75}{0.5}$.\nSubstituting: L = 150 cm. This is less than 200 cm, so the tube does not fill completely.\nSo the length of mercury along the tube is 150 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"If the fluid is at rest everywhere, Bernoulli's equation applied between points 1 and 2 reduces to:","image":"","options":["$P_1 = P_2$ for all points","$P_1 - P_2 = \\rho g (h_2 - h_1)$","$P_1 - P_2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho (v_2^2 - v_1^2)$","$P_1 - P_2 = \\rho g (h_1 - h_2)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"At rest, $v_1 = v_2 = 0$, so both kinetic terms vanish.\nThe equation becomes $P_1 + \\rho g h_1 = P_2 + \\rho g h_2$, so $P_1 - P_2 = \\rho g(h_2 - h_1)$.\nThis is the hydrostatic result $P_2 - P_1 = \\rho g h$: the lower point has the higher pressure.\nOption (4) has the sign reversed. Option (1) is true only for points at the same height.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In a hydraulic lift, the smaller piston has a cross-sectional area of $10\\ \\text{cm}^2$ and the larger piston has a cross-sectional area of $500\\ \\text{cm}^2$. A force of $200$ N is applied on the smaller piston. Neglecting friction and the weights of the pistons, what is the maximum load the larger piston can support?","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^4$ N","$4.0$ N","$4.0 \\times 10^2$ N","$1.0 \\times 10^5$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A_1 = 10\\ \\text{cm}^2$, $A_2 = 500\\ \\text{cm}^2$, $F_1 = 200$ N.\nFormula: The pressure $\\frac{F_1}{A_1}$ is transmitted undiminished to the larger piston (Pascal's law), so $F_2 = \\frac{F_1 A_2}{A_1}$.\nSubstituting: $F_2 = 200 \\times \\frac{500}{10} = 200 \\times 50$. Both areas are in the same unit, so the ratio needs no conversion.\nSo the load supported is $F_2 = 1.0 \\times 10^4$ N.\nUsing the ratio upside down gives 4 N. That is the tempting error.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a steady flow, two streamlines can never intersect each other.\nReason (R): If two streamlines crossed, a fluid particle arriving at the crossing point could move along either of them, so the velocity there would not be uniquely defined in time.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. In steady flow the map of streamlines is fixed in time, and NCERT states that no two streamlines can cross.\nR is true. At a crossing point the tangent would give two directions of velocity. An oncoming particle could then go either way, and the flow would not be steady.\nR is exactly the reasoning NCERT gives for A, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"An incompressible liquid flows steadily through a horizontal pipe. The pipe narrows from an internal diameter of 6 cm to 2 cm. If the speed in the wider part is 0.5 m/s, what is the speed in the narrower part?","image":"","options":["4.5 m/s","1.5 m/s","0.056 m/s","0.167 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $d_1 = 6$ cm, $d_2 = 2$ cm, $v_1 = 0.5$ m/s.\nFormula: $A_1v_1 = A_2v_2$ with $A = \\frac{\\pi d^2}{4}$, so $v_2 = v_1\\left(\\frac{d_1}{d_2}\\right)^2$.\nSubstituting: $v_2 = 0.5 \\times (3)^2$.\nSo $v_2 = 4.5$ m/s.\nUsing the diameter ratio instead of the area ratio gives 1.5 m/s, which is the trap.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Bernoulli's equation $P + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2 + \\rho gh$ = constant along a streamline is derived from the work–energy theorem. How many of the following are conditions required for it to hold as derived in NCERT?\n(i) The fluid is incompressible\n(ii) The fluid is non-viscous\n(iii) The flow is steady (streamline)\n(iv) The flow may be turbulent\n(v) The pipe must be horizontal\n(vi) The fluid must be at rest","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Required: the elastic energy of a compressible fluid is not accounted for.\n(ii) Required: viscosity would dissipate energy as heat.\n(iii) Required: the equation fails when velocity and pressure fluctuate.\n(iv) Not a condition: the equation does not hold for turbulent flow.\n(v) Not required: the $\\rho gh$ term handles height changes.\n(vi) Not required: rest is only a special case, which reduces the equation to $P_1 - P_2 = \\rho g(h_2 - h_1)$.\nThat gives 3 conditions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A thin plate of area 0.10 m$^2$ is pulled horizontally at a constant speed of 0.20 m/s between two large fixed parallel plates. The gap above the plate is 1.0 mm wide and filled with oil of viscosity 0.50 Pa s. The gap below is 2.0 mm wide and filled with oil of viscosity 0.30 Pa s. Assuming a linear velocity profile in each gap, what force is required?","image":"","options":["13 N","10 N","3 N","7 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 0.10 m$^2$, v = 0.20 m/s, $\\eta_1 = 0.50$ Pa s, $l_1 = 10^{-3}$ m, $\\eta_2 = 0.30$ Pa s, $l_2 = 2\\times10^{-3}$ m.\nBoth oil layers oppose the motion, so their viscous forces add.\nFormula: $F = Av\\left(\\frac{\\eta_1}{l_1} + \\frac{\\eta_2}{l_2}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $F = 0.02 \\times (500 + 150) = 0.02 \\times 650$.\nSo F = 13 N.\nOption (4) wrongly subtracts the two forces instead of adding them.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are conditions or assumptions required for Bernoulli's equation $P + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v^2 + \\rho g h = \\text{constant}$ to hold exactly?\n(i) The fluid is non-viscous.\n(ii) The fluid is incompressible.\n(iii) The flow is steady.\n(iv) The flow is turbulent.\n(v) The density of the fluid changes along the pipe.\n(vi) No energy is lost to internal friction.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Required: viscosity dissipates energy as heat.\n(ii) Required: elastic energy of compression is not included in the equation.\n(iii) Required: the equation applies along streamlines of steady flow.\n(iv) Not required: in turbulent flow, velocity and pressure fluctuate in time, so the equation fails.\n(v) Not required: the derivation takes $\\rho$ as constant.\n(vi) Required: this is the energy-conservation assumption.\nThe required conditions are (i), (ii), (iii) and (vi), so the count is 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a Torricelli mercury barometer, the pressure at the top of the mercury column inside the tube is taken as nearly zero.\nReason (R): The space above the mercury column contains only mercury vapour, whose pressure is negligibly small.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT takes the pressure at point A (top of the column) as 0.\nR is true: the closed space above the mercury holds only mercury vapour. Its pressure is so small that it can be neglected.\nR explains A: because the vapour pressure is negligible, the top of the column is effectively at zero pressure. This is why $P_a = \\rho g h$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The same small metal ball dropped into machine oil attains a larger terminal velocity when the oil is at 38 °C than when it is at 16 °C.\nReason (R): The coefficient of viscosity of a liquid decreases as its temperature rises.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. In liquids, molecules become more mobile when heated, so $\\eta$ falls. For machine oil, $\\eta$ drops from 113 mPl at 16 °C to 34 mPl at 38 °C.\nA is true. Since $v_t = \\frac{2a^2(\\rho-\\sigma)g}{9\\eta}$, a smaller $\\eta$ gives a larger $v_t$. The small change in oil density is negligible by comparison.\nR therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the force exerted by a liquid at rest on the surface of a submerged object is correct?","image":"","options":["On an inclined surface, the force can have a component parallel to the surface","The force is always normal to the surface; a parallel component would, by Newton's third law, make the liquid flow along the surface","The force is normal only on horizontal surfaces, such as the base of the container","The force is normal on the container walls but not on objects immersed in the liquid"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Suppose the liquid pushed on the surface with a component parallel to it.\nBy Newton's third law, the surface would push back on the liquid along the same direction, and the liquid would flow.\nA liquid at rest does not flow, so its force must be perpendicular to every surface it touches.\nThis holds for any orientation, and for walls as well as immersed bodies. That rules out options (1), (3) and (4).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical small spherical raindrops, each falling through air with a terminal velocity of 5.0 cm/s, merge to form a single spherical drop. Assume Stokes' law holds. What is the terminal velocity of the combined drop?","image":"","options":["7.9 cm/s","10.0 cm/s","6.3 cm/s","20.0 cm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: two drops of radius $r$ merge, and each has $v = 5.0$ cm/s.\nVolume is conserved: $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3 = 2 \\times \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$, so $R = 2^{1/3} r$.\nFormula: $v_t \\propto a^2$, so $v' = v \\left(\\frac{R}{r}\\right)^2 = 2^{2/3} v$.\nSubstituting: $v' = 1.587 \\times 5.0$.\nSo $v' \\approx 7.9$ cm/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Streamline\n(B) Volume flux\n(C) Critical speed\n(D) Equation of continuity\nList-II:\n(I) Product $Av$, constant along a pipe for an incompressible fluid\n(II) Statement of conservation of mass in fluid flow\n(III) Curve whose tangent at any point gives the direction of fluid velocity\n(IV) Speed beyond which steady flow becomes turbulent","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A streamline is a curve whose tangent gives the velocity direction, so A-III.\n(B) Volume flux, or flow rate, is $Av$, so B-I.\n(C) Above the critical speed the flow turns turbulent, so C-IV.\n(D) The equation of continuity expresses conservation of mass, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A sphere of radius 1.0 mm moves slowly through water at 20 °C with a speed of 0.10 m/s. The viscosity of water at this temperature is $1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ Pa s. What is the viscous drag on the sphere?","image":"","options":["$1.88 \\times 10^{-6}$ N","$0.63 \\times 10^{-6}$ N","$3.77 \\times 10^{-6}$ N","$18.8 \\times 10^{-6}$ N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\eta = 1.0\\times10^{-3}$ Pa s, $a = 1.0\\times10^{-3}$ m, $v = 0.10$ m/s.\nFormula: $F = 6\\pi\\eta a v$.\nSubstituting: $F = 6\\pi \\times 10^{-3} \\times 10^{-3} \\times 0.10$.\nSo $F \\approx 1.88\\times10^{-6}$ N.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following work mainly on the principle that pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted undiminished to every part of it?\n(i) Hydraulic lift\n(ii) Hydraulic brakes of a car\n(iii) Dynamic lift on an aeroplane wing\n(iv) Swing of a spinning cricket ball\n(v) Car lift in which compressed air pushes a small piston connected to a larger one\n(vi) Mercury barometer","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Pascal's law: the small piston's pressure acts on the large piston.\n(ii) Pascal's law: pedal pressure reaches all four wheel cylinders through brake oil.\n(iii) Not Pascal's law: wing lift comes from Bernoulli's principle.\n(iv) Not Pascal's law: the swing is the Magnus effect, a Bernoulli phenomenon.\n(v) Pascal's law: this is the car lift of Example 9.6.\n(vi) Not Pascal's law: the barometer balances atmospheric pressure against $\\rho gh$ of mercury.\nThat gives 3 devices.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a steady flow, the tangent drawn to a streamline at any point gives the direction of the fluid velocity at that point.\nStatement II: In a steady flow, two streamlines may intersect at a point, provided the fluid speed along both streamlines is the same at that point.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A streamline is defined as a curve whose tangent at any point lies along the fluid velocity at that point.\nStatement II is incorrect. If two streamlines crossed, the two tangents at the crossing point would point in two different directions.\nAn arriving particle would then have two possible velocities and could go either way, so the flow could not be steady. This is true whatever the speeds are.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the derivation of Bernoulli's equation for fluid flowing from region 1 (pressure $P_1$) to region 2 (pressure $P_2$), with volume $\\Delta V$ crossing each end in time $\\Delta t$.\nStatement I: The net work done on the fluid by the pressure forces at the two ends is $(P_1 - P_2)\\Delta V$.\nStatement II: The pressure force at the outlet end does work $+P_2\\Delta V$ on the fluid, because the fluid moves in the direction of this force.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"At the outlet, the surrounding fluid pushes backward on our element with force $P_2A_2$, opposite to the motion.\nThe fluid does work $P_2\\Delta V$ on the surroundings, so the work done on the fluid is $-P_2\\Delta V$. Statement II is incorrect.\nThe work at the inlet is $+P_1\\Delta V$, so the net work is $(P_1 - P_2)\\Delta V$. Statement I is correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A U-tube of uniform cross-section contains mercury (relative density 13.6), with both arms open to the atmosphere. Water is poured slowly into the left arm until the water column is 27.2 cm long. What is the vertical distance between the top of the water in the left arm and the mercury surface in the right arm?","image":"","options":["25.2 cm","26.2 cm","24.2 cm","27.2 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Mercury falls by x in the left arm and rises by x in the right arm (uniform tube), so the mercury level difference is 2x.\nTake the level of the left mercury surface. Equating pressures there: $\\rho_w g(27.2) = \\rho_{Hg} g(2x)$, so $2x = \\frac{27.2}{13.6} = 2$ cm and x = 1 cm.\nThe water top lies 27.2 − 1 = 26.2 cm above the original mercury level.\nThe right mercury surface lies 1 cm above the original level.\nThe difference is 26.2 − 1 = 25.2 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a liquid at rest in an open container. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Pressure at a point in the liquid has no direction associated with it.\n(ii) Pressure is the same at all points of the liquid lying in the same horizontal plane.\n(iii) For the same depth of liquid, the pressure at the bottom of a vessel with a wider base is larger.\n(iv) The gauge pressure at depth h is $\\rho g h$.\n(v) The force exerted by the liquid on a submerged surface can have a component parallel to that surface.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: pressure acts equally in all directions, so it is a scalar.\n(ii) Correct: otherwise a net horizontal force would make the fluid flow.\n(iii) Incorrect: pressure depends only on depth, not on base area.\n(iv) Correct: $P - P_a = \\rho g h$.\n(v) Incorrect: a tangential component would make the fluid flow parallel to the surface (Newton's third law), so the force must be normal.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A garden hose has an internal cross-sectional area of 2.0 $cm^2$. Water moves through the hose at 72 m/min. The hose is fitted with a shower nozzle that has 30 circular holes, each of diameter 0.80 mm. Assuming steady flow of incompressible water, what is the speed with which water emerges from each hole?","image":"","options":["15.9 m/s","3.98 m/s","477 m/s","955 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 2.0\\times10^{-4}$ $m^2$ and $v = 72$ m/min = 1.2 m/s. There are 30 holes, each of radius 0.40 mm.\nFormula: by continuity, the volume flux is conserved: $Av = N\\pi r^2 v'$.\nFlux: $2.0\\times10^{-4}\\times1.2 = 2.4\\times10^{-4}$ $m^3/s$.\nTotal hole area: $30\\times\\pi(0.40\\times10^{-3})^2 = 1.51\\times10^{-5}$ $m^2$.\nSubstituting: $v' = \\frac{2.4\\times10^{-4}}{1.51\\times10^{-5}} \\approx 15.9$ m/s.\nSo the jet speed is about 15.9 m/s. Using the diameter as the radius gives 3.98 m/s, and ignoring the 30 holes gives 477 m/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Water (density $1000$ kg m$^{-3}$) flows steadily through an inclined pipe. At point 1 the cross-sectional area is $20$ cm$^2$, the speed is $3$ m/s and the absolute pressure is $2.00 \\times 10^5$ Pa. Point 2 is $2.0$ m higher than point 1 and has a cross-sectional area of $10$ cm$^2$. Treat water as an ideal fluid and take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$. What is the absolute pressure at point 2?","image":"","options":["$1.665 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$1.865 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$1.800 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$2.335 \\times 10^5$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A_1 = 20$ cm$^2$, $A_2 = 10$ cm$^2$, $v_1 = 3$ m/s, $h_2 - h_1 = 2$ m.\nContinuity: $v_2 = \\frac{A_1 v_1}{A_2} = 6$ m/s.\nFormula: $P_1 - P_2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho(v_2^2 - v_1^2) + \\rho g(h_2 - h_1)$.\nSubstituting: $P_1 - P_2 = \\frac{1}{2}\\times1000\\times27 + 1000\\times10\\times2 = 13500 + 20000 = 33500$ Pa.\nSo $P_2 = 2.00\\times10^5 - 0.335\\times10^5 = 1.665\\times10^5$ Pa.\nIgnoring the height term would wrongly give $1.865\\times10^5$ Pa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Two water-filled syringes (without needles) are joined by a tightly fitting water-filled rubber tube. Their piston diameters are 2.0 cm and 6.0 cm. If the smaller piston is pushed in by 15 cm, how far does the larger piston move out? (Treat water as incompressible.)","image":"","options":["0.56 cm","1.67 cm","5.0 cm","45 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $d_1$ = 2.0 cm, $d_2$ = 6.0 cm, $L_1$ = 15 cm.\nFormula: water is incompressible, so the volume pushed in equals the volume pushed out: $L_1A_1 = L_2A_2$. This gives $L_2 = L_1\\left(\\frac{d_1}{d_2}\\right)^2$.\nSubstituting: $L_2 = 15 \\times (2/6)^2 = 15/9$.\nSo $L_2 \\approx$ 1.67 cm. The larger piston moves less, while it exerts a larger force.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In a car lift, compressed air pushes on a small piston of radius 4.0 cm. The pressure is transmitted to a large piston of radius 20 cm, which carries a car of mass 1200 kg. What minimum force $F_1$ must act on the small piston to support the car? (g = 10 m/s$^2$; ignore the piston weights.)","image":"","options":["96 N","480 N","2400 N","60000 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: m = 1200 kg, so $F_2 = mg$ = 12000 N; $r_1$ = 4.0 cm, $r_2$ = 20 cm.\nFormula: $F_1 = F_2\\frac{A_1}{A_2} = F_2\\left(\\frac{r_1}{r_2}\\right)^2$.\nSubstituting: $F_1 = 12000 \\times (4/20)^2 = 12000/25$.\nSo $F_1$ = 480 N. Using the ratio of radii instead of areas gives 2400 N, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A flat plate of area 0.20 m$^2$ rests on a 0.50 mm thick layer of liquid of viscosity 0.80 Pa s spread on a fixed horizontal surface. What horizontal force is needed to move the plate at a constant speed of 0.10 m/s?","image":"","options":["32 N","3.2 N","320 N","16 N"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 0.20$ m$^2$, $l = 5\\times10^{-4}$ m, $\\eta = 0.80$ Pa s, $v = 0.10$ m/s.\nFormula: $\\eta = \\frac{F/A}{v/l}$, so $F = \\frac{\\eta A v}{l}$.\nSubstituting: $F = \\frac{0.80 \\times 0.20 \\times 0.10}{5\\times10^{-4}}$.\nSo F = 32 N.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A narrow clean glass capillary tube is dipped vertically into a trough of mercury. Which of the following correctly describes what is observed?","image":"","options":["Mercury rises in the tube and its meniscus is concave, because the angle of contact is acute","Mercury rises in the tube and its meniscus is convex, because the angle of contact is obtuse","Mercury level in the tube is depressed below the outside level and its meniscus is convex, because the angle of contact is obtuse","Mercury level in the tube stays equal to the outside level, because mercury does not wet glass"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The angle of contact of mercury with glass is obtuse, so mercury does not wet glass and its meniscus is convex.\nIn $h = \\frac{2S\\cos\\theta}{\\rho g a}$, $\\cos\\theta$ is negative for an obtuse angle, so $h$ is negative. This means the mercury level inside the tube falls below the outside level (capillary depression).\nNot wetting does not mean there is no capillary effect. The curved surface still produces a pressure difference, which is why the level is depressed rather than equal.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel ball of radius 1.0 mm falls through glycerine and reaches terminal velocity. The density of steel is $7.8 \\times 10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$, the density of glycerine is $1.26 \\times 10^{3}$ kg m$^{-3}$ and the viscosity of glycerine is 0.83 Pa s. Take g = 10 m/s$^2$. What is the terminal velocity of the ball?","image":"","options":["1.75 cm/s","2.09 cm/s","3.50 cm/s","0.88 cm/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: a = $10^{-3}$ m, $\\rho = 7800$ kg m$^{-3}$, $\\sigma = 1260$ kg m$^{-3}$, $\\eta = 0.83$ Pa s, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: $v_t = \\frac{2a^2(\\rho-\\sigma)g}{9\\eta}$\nSubstituting: $v_t = \\frac{2 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 6540 \\times 10}{9 \\times 0.83} = \\frac{0.1308}{7.47}$ m/s\nSo $v_t \\approx 0.0175$ m/s = 1.75 cm/s.\nThe value 2.09 cm/s is what you get if you ignore buoyancy and use $\\rho$ instead of $(\\rho-\\sigma)$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"An open water tank has a small hole in its side, 5.0 m below the free surface of the water. The tank's cross-section is very large compared with the hole. What is the speed of efflux of water through the hole? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["5 m/s","10 m/s","50 m/s","100 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: depth of hole below free surface h = 5.0 m, g = 10 m/s$^2$. The tank is open and $A_2 \\gg A_1$.\nFormula: Torricelli's law, $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$.\nSubstituting: $v = \\sqrt{2 \\times 10 \\times 5} = \\sqrt{100}$.\nSo v = 10 m/s. Option (3), 50 m/s, comes from forgetting the square root.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The pressure inside an air bubble formed within water is greater than the pressure of the water just outside it.\nReason (R): Across a curved liquid–gas interface, the pressure on the convex side is always higher than that on the concave side.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. For an air bubble in a liquid, the excess pressure is $P_i - P_o = \\frac{2S}{r}$, so the inside pressure is higher.\nR is false. NCERT states that the concave side (the inside of the curvature) has the higher pressure, not the convex side.\nFor a bubble, the inside is the concave side, which is why its pressure is greater.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A diver is 5.0 m below the surface of a freshwater lake. Taking the density of water as 1000 kg m$^{-3}$ and g = 10 m/s$^2$, what is the gauge pressure on the diver?","image":"","options":["50 kPa","151 kPa","5 kPa","101 kPa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 5.0 m, $\\rho$ = 1000 kg m$^{-3}$, g = 10 m/s$^2$.\nFormula: gauge pressure $P_g = P - P_a = \\rho g h$.\nSubstituting: $P_g = 1000 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 5.0 \\times 10^4$ Pa.\nSo the gauge pressure = 50 kPa. The value 151 kPa is the absolute pressure, which adds atmospheric pressure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A wide closed tank holds water (density $1000\\ kg\\ m^{-3}$). A small hole is made in its side 1.8 m below the water surface. The air trapped above the water is kept at a pressure $3.2 \\times 10^4$ Pa above atmospheric pressure. What is the speed of efflux of water from the hole? (Take $g = 10\\ m/s^2$; neglect viscosity.)","image":"","options":["10 m/s","6 m/s","8 m/s","14 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 1.8 m, $P - P_a = 3.2 \\times 10^4$ Pa, $\\rho = 1000\\ kg\\ m^{-3}$. The tank is wide, so the speed at the top is about zero.\nFormula (Bernoulli): $v = \\sqrt{2gh + \\frac{2(P - P_a)}{\\rho}}$.\nSubstituting: $2gh = 36$ and $\\frac{2(P - P_a)}{\\rho} = 64$, so $v = \\sqrt{100}$.\n6 m/s uses only Torricelli's term. 14 m/s wrongly adds the two speeds instead of the squares.\nSo v = 10 m/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A fluid has no definite shape of its own.\n(ii) Like a gas, a liquid fills the entire volume of its container.\n(iii) The shearing stress of fluids is about a million times smaller than that of solids.\n(iv) Solids and liquids have much lower compressibility than gases.\n(v) The volume of a liquid does not depend on the external pressure at all.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. A fluid takes the shape of its container.\n(ii) Incorrect. Only a gas fills the whole container; a liquid has a fixed volume.\n(iii) Correct. NCERT states this figure.\n(iv) Correct. The volume change of solids and liquids with pressure is rather small.\n(v) Incorrect. Volume does depend on pressure. A 'fixed volume' means the volume at atmospheric pressure.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A steel ball of radius 1.5 mm falls through a tank of oil with a steady speed of 5.0 cm/s. The density of steel is $7.8 \\times 10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$ and the density of the oil is $0.9 \\times 10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$. Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$. What is the coefficient of viscosity of the oil?","image":"","options":["0.69 Pa s","0.35 Pa s","1.38 Pa s","0.078 Pa s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $a = 1.5\\times10^{-3}$ m, $v_t = 0.05$ m/s, $\\rho-\\sigma = 6.9\\times10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$.\nFormula: $\\eta = \\frac{2a^2(\\rho-\\sigma)g}{9v_t}$.\nSubstituting: $\\eta = \\frac{2(2.25\\times10^{-6})(6.9\\times10^3)(10)}{9(0.05)} = \\frac{0.3105}{0.45}$.\nSo $\\eta \\approx 0.69$ Pa s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A drop of water rests on a waxy solid surface in air. The three interfacial tensions are: liquid–air $S_{la} = 0.0727$ N/m, solid–air $S_{sa} = 0.0250$ N/m and solid–liquid $S_{sl} = 0.06135$ N/m. Using the equilibrium of surface forces at the line of contact, what is the angle of contact $\\theta$?","image":"","options":["60°","120°","30°","150°"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $S_{la}$ = 0.0727 N/m, $S_{sa}$ = 0.0250 N/m, $S_{sl}$ = 0.06135 N/m.\nFormula: $S_{la}\\cos\\theta + S_{sl} = S_{sa}$, so $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{S_{sa} - S_{sl}}{S_{la}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{0.0250 - 0.06135}{0.0727} = \\frac{-0.03635}{0.0727} = -0.5$.\nSo $\\theta$ = 120°. This is obtuse, as expected because $S_{sl} > S_{la}$. Water does not wet the waxy surface and forms droplets.\nDropping the negative sign would wrongly give 60°.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following gives the dimensional formula of pressure?","image":"","options":["$[MLT^{-2}]$","$[ML^{2}T^{-2}]$","$[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$","$[ML^{-2}T^{-2}]$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Pressure = normal force / area.\nForce has dimensions $[MLT^{-2}]$ and area has $[L^{2}]$.\nSo pressure has $[MLT^{-2}]/[L^{2}] = [ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$.\n$[MLT^{-2}]$ is force itself and $[ML^{2}T^{-2}]$ is energy, so those options fail.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Match each observation in List-I with the principle in List-II that best explains it.\nList-I:\n(A) Liquid flows out of a small hole in an open tank with speed $\\sqrt{2gh}$\n(B) Water speeds up where a pipe narrows\n(C) A raindrop finally falls with constant speed\n(D) Braking effort is equal on all four wheels of a car\nList-II:\n(I) Pascal's law\n(II) Stokes' law\n(III) Equation of continuity\n(IV) Bernoulli's principle","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Torricelli's law comes from Bernoulli's equation with $P = P_a$ at both the surface and the hole, so A-IV.\n(B) $Av$ = constant, so a smaller area means a larger speed. This is the equation of continuity, B-III.\n(C) Terminal velocity is reached when the Stokes drag $6\\pi\\eta av$ plus buoyancy balances the weight, so C-II.\n(D) Pedal pressure is transmitted equally to all wheel cylinders by Pascal's law, so D-I.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A soap bubble of radius 1.0 cm floats in air. An air bubble of the same radius, 1.0 cm, is formed just below the surface of the same soap solution. The surface tension of the solution is $2.5 \\times 10^{-2}$ N m$^{-1}$. By how much does the excess pressure inside the soap bubble exceed the excess pressure inside the air bubble?","image":"","options":["5 Pa","10 Pa","2.5 Pa","15 Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $S = 2.5 \\times 10^{-2}$ N/m and $r = 1.0 \\times 10^{-2}$ m.\nFormula: A soap bubble in air has two surfaces, so $\\Delta P = \\frac{4S}{r}$. An air bubble in liquid has one surface, so $\\Delta P = \\frac{2S}{r}$.\nSubstituting: the soap bubble gives $\\frac{4(0.025)}{0.01} = 10$ Pa. The air bubble gives $\\frac{2(0.025)}{0.01} = 5$ Pa.\nSo the difference = 10 − 5 = 5 Pa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The surface tension of a liquid usually decreases as its temperature rises.\n(ii) Of the liquids listed at 20 °C in NCERT Table 9.3 (ethanol, water, mercury), mercury has the largest surface tension.\n(iii) Of all the liquids in Table 9.3, helium (at –270 °C) has the smallest surface tension.\n(iv) Surface tension is a property of a single fluid alone and does not depend on the medium on the other side of the interface.\n(v) If the molecules of two materials in contact attract each other, the interfacial surface energy is reduced.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: like viscosity, surface tension usually falls with temperature.\n(ii) Correct: mercury has 0.4355 N/m, compared with 0.0727 N/m for water and 0.0227 N/m for ethanol.\n(iii) Correct: helium's value is 0.000239 N/m, the lowest in the table.\n(iv) Incorrect: surface energy belongs to the interface and depends on both materials.\n(v) Correct: mutual attraction lowers the interfacial energy.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Twenty-seven identical spherical raindrops fall through air, each with the same terminal velocity. They coalesce into a single spherical drop, which then reaches its own terminal velocity. Assume Stokes' law holds and neglect the buoyancy of air. What is the ratio of the viscous drag force on the big drop at its terminal velocity to the drag force on one small drop at its terminal velocity?","image":"","options":["27","9","3","81"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Volume is conserved: $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi R^3 = 27 \\times \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$, so R = 3r.\nFrom $v_t = \\frac{2a^2\\rho g}{9\\eta}$, $v_t \\propto a^2$. So V = 9v.\nUsing $F = 6\\pi\\eta a v$, the ratio is $\\frac{F_{big}}{F_{small}} = \\frac{3r \\times 9v}{r \\times v} = 27$.\nCheck: at terminal velocity the drag equals the weight, and the weight scales with volume, which is 27 times larger.\nThe option 9 is the velocity ratio, not the force ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"According to the viscosity data in NCERT (Table 9.2), which of the following fluids has the LOWEST coefficient of viscosity?","image":"","options":["Water at 100 °C","Blood at 37 °C","Air at 0 °C","Machine oil at 38 °C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Table 9.2 lists these viscosities:\nWater at 100 °C: 0.3 mPl\nBlood at 37 °C: 2.7 mPl\nMachine oil at 38 °C: 34 mPl\nAir at 0 °C: 0.017 mPl\nAir, a gas, is far less viscous than any of the liquids.\nThe tempting choice is hot water. Its viscosity falls on heating, but 0.3 mPl is still about 18 times that of air.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A raindrop falling from a cloud first accelerates and later descends with a constant velocity.\nStatement II: The viscous force on the drop increases with its speed until the viscous force plus the buoyant force equals the force of gravity on the drop.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The drop starts from low speed and gravity accelerates it. Once the forces balance, it moves with terminal velocity.\nStatement II is correct. Stokes' drag $6\\pi\\eta a v$ grows with $v$. Balance is reached when drag plus buoyancy equals the force due to gravity.\nAt that point the net force is zero, so the acceleration is zero.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The speed of efflux of a liquid from a small hole at depth h below the surface of a wide open tank is $\\sqrt{2gh}$, the same for water and for mercury.\nStatement II: For a tank much wider than the hole, the speed of efflux depends on the area of the hole.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. In Bernoulli's equation every term contains $\\rho$, so the density cancels and $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$ for any ideal liquid. This is the speed of a body falling freely through height h.\nStatement II: Incorrect. When $A_2 \\gg A_1$, the surface speed is taken as zero, and the hole area does not appear in $\\sqrt{2gh}$. The hole area affects the volume flow rate $A_1 v_1$, not the speed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT Table 9.2, the viscosity of machine oil is 113 mPl at 16 °C and 34 mPl at 38 °C. Which general behaviour does this illustrate?","image":"","options":["Viscosity of liquids increases with temperature","Viscosity of liquids decreases with temperature","Viscosity of liquids does not depend on temperature","Viscosity of liquids first increases and then decreases with temperature"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The viscosity of the oil falls from 113 mPl to 34 mPl as the temperature rises.\nIn a liquid, a higher temperature makes the molecules more mobile, so the internal friction decreases.\nGases behave the opposite way: their viscosity increases with temperature.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When the temperature rises, the coefficient of viscosity of a liquid decreases, while that of a gas increases.\nStatement II: The surface tension of a liquid usually increases as its temperature rises.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In a liquid, a higher temperature makes the molecules more mobile, so $\\eta$ falls. In a gas, a higher temperature increases the random molecular motion, so $\\eta$ rises.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT notes that, like the viscosity of a liquid, surface tension usually falls with temperature.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the temperature of a gas is raised, its coefficient of viscosity increases, while that of a liquid decreases.\nReason (R): A rise in temperature increases the random motion of gas atoms, but makes liquid atoms more mobile.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the viscosity of liquids decreases with temperature, while that of gases increases.\nR is true. In a liquid, heating makes atoms more mobile, so $\\eta$ falls. In a gas, heating increases the random motion of atoms, so $\\eta$ rises.\nR gives the reason for both trends stated in A, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"The surface tension of the liquid–air interface is $S$ in every case below. Match each situation in List-I with the excess pressure (inside minus outside) in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Liquid drop of radius $r$ in air\n(B) Soap bubble of radius $r$ in air\n(C) Air bubble of radius $2r$ inside the liquid\n(D) Soap bubble of radius $8r$ in air\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{S}{r}$\n(II) $\\frac{S}{2r}$\n(III) $\\frac{4S}{r}$\n(IV) $\\frac{2S}{r}$","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A drop has one surface, so the excess pressure is $\\frac{2S}{r}$ (IV).\n(B) A soap bubble has two surfaces, so the excess pressure is $\\frac{4S}{r}$ (III).\n(C) An air bubble in a liquid has one surface: $\\frac{2S}{2r} = \\frac{S}{r}$ (I).\n(D) A soap bubble of radius $8r$ has two surfaces: $\\frac{4S}{8r} = \\frac{S}{2r}$ (II).\nOption (3) wrongly treats the air bubble as having two surfaces.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A soap bubble of radius 2.0 cm is blown in air. The surface tension of the soap solution is $2.5 \\times 10^{-2}$ N/m. What is the excess pressure inside the bubble?","image":"","options":["2.5 Pa","5.0 Pa","10 Pa","1.25 Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: r = 0.02 m, S = 0.025 N/m.\nFormula: a soap bubble has two surfaces, so $P_i - P_o = \\frac{4S}{r}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{4 \\times 0.025}{0.02}$ = 5.0 Pa.\nUsing $\\frac{2S}{r}$ (the drop formula) would wrongly give 2.5 Pa.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Pressure is a scalar quantity even though it is defined as force per unit area.\nReason (R): Only the component of force normal to the area enters the definition, and at a point in a fluid at rest the pressure is the same in all directions.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that pressure is a scalar with no direction assigned to it.\nR is true. The numerator is the normal component of force, not the force vector. By Pascal's law, the pressure at a point in a fluid at rest is the same in all directions.\nSince pressure acts equally in every direction and the force on any area is simply normal to it, no direction can be attached to pressure. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a hydraulic lift, a small force applied on the small piston can support a heavy car placed on the large piston.\nReason (R): The pressure on the large piston is greater than the pressure on the small piston by the factor $\\frac{A_2}{A_1}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The force on the large piston is $F_2 = F_1\\frac{A_2}{A_1}$, which is much larger than $F_1$ when $A_2 \\gg A_1$.\nR is false. The pressure is the same on both pistons, because it is transmitted undiminished through the liquid.\nThe factor $\\frac{A_2}{A_1}$ multiplies the force, not the pressure. The force is larger only because the same pressure acts on a larger area.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A small steel ball is dropped from rest into a tall jar of glycerine. After some time it moves down with a constant terminal velocity. Which of the following statements about the ball at terminal velocity is correct?","image":"","options":["The viscous force on the ball is zero because the ball no longer accelerates.","The viscous force plus the buoyant force on the ball equals the weight of the ball.","The viscous force alone equals the weight of the ball.","The buoyant force on the ball has become zero."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The forces on the ball are its weight (down), the buoyant force (up) and the viscous drag (up, opposing the motion).\nTerminal velocity means zero acceleration, so the net force is zero.\nSo weight = viscous force + buoyant force, i.e. $6\\pi\\eta a v_t + \\frac{4}{3}\\pi a^3\\sigma g = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi a^3\\rho g$.\n(1) fails: drag is largest at terminal velocity. (3) fails: it ignores buoyancy. (4) fails: buoyancy does not depend on speed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The pressure of air inside a small air bubble in water is greater than the pressure of the water just outside it.\nReason (R): Across a curved liquid–gas interface, the pressure on the concave side is higher than the pressure on the convex side.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The excess pressure inside an air bubble in a liquid is $\\frac{2S}{r}$.\nR is true. NCERT states that the concave (inner) side of a liquid–gas interface is at higher pressure.\nFor an air bubble, the air lies on the concave side of the water surface, so R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Water flows steadily through a horizontal pipe. At one section the internal diameter is 4.0 cm and the flow speed is 0.50 m/s. Further along, the pipe narrows to an internal diameter of 2.0 cm. Treating water as incompressible, what is the flow speed in the narrow section?","image":"","options":["1.0 m/s","2.0 m/s","0.25 m/s","4.0 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $d_1 = 4.0$ cm, $v_1 = 0.50$ m/s, $d_2 = 2.0$ cm.\nFormula: equation of continuity, $A_1v_1 = A_2v_2$, with $A \\propto d^2$.\nSubstituting: $v_2 = v_1\\left(\\frac{d_1}{d_2}\\right)^2 = 0.50 \\times 4 = 2.0$ m/s.\nTaking the diameter ratio instead of its square gives 1.0 m/s, which is wrong.\nSo $v_2$ = 2.0 m/s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"An ideal hydraulic lift has a small piston of area 0.003 $m^2$ and a large piston of area 0.15 $m^2$. A car of mass 1500 kg rests on the large piston. How much work must be done by the force on the small piston to raise the car slowly through 2.0 cm? (g = 10 m/s$^2$; liquid incompressible; neglect friction and the piston masses.)","image":"","options":["6 J","300 J","3000 J","15000 J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $F_2 = mg$ = 15000 N, $A_2/A_1$ = 0.15/0.003 = 50, $L_2$ = 0.02 m.\nForce on the small piston: $F_1 = F_2\\frac{A_1}{A_2} = 15000/50$ = 300 N.\nDistance moved by the small piston: $L_1 = L_2\\frac{A_2}{A_1} = 0.02 \\times 50$ = 1.0 m.\nWork: $W = F_1L_1 = 300 \\times 1.0$ = 300 J. This equals $mgh$ = 15000 × 0.02 J, so the lift multiplies force but not energy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about fluids and pressure:\n(i) Pressure has the same dimensions as stress, $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$.\n(ii) The relative density of aluminium is $2.7\\ \\text{kg m}^{-3}$.\n(iii) A fluid at rest can exert a force with a component parallel to the surface of an object submerged in it.\n(iv) The shearing stress of fluids is about a million times smaller than that of solids.\n(v) Liquids, like solids, have much lower compressibility than gases.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: pressure is normal force per area, with SI unit N m$^{-2}$ (pascal), so its dimensions are $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$.\n(ii) Incorrect: relative density is a ratio of two densities, so it is dimensionless. It is 2.7 with no unit; the density of aluminium is $2.7\\times10^3$ kg m$^{-3}$.\n(iii) Incorrect: a parallel component would make the fluid flow along the surface (Newton's third law). A fluid at rest therefore exerts only normal force.\n(iv) Correct, as stated in NCERT.\n(v) Correct: the volume of a liquid changes very little with external pressure.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the flow of an incompressible fluid is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In steady flow, the velocity of the fluid at any fixed point does not change with time.","The equation of continuity, $Av$ = constant, expresses conservation of mass.","In a region where the streamlines are crowded together, the flow speed is larger.","In steady flow, two streamlines may cross at a point where the flow speed is maximum."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Option (1) is correct: this is the definition of steady flow.\nOption (2) is correct: for an incompressible fluid, mass flowing in equals mass flowing out, which gives $Av$ = constant.\nOption (3) is correct: crowded streamlines mean a smaller cross-section, so $v$ is larger.\nOption (4) is incorrect: if two streamlines crossed, a particle at that point would have two possible velocities, and the flow could not be steady.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A viscous liquid flows steadily (laminar flow) through a horizontal cylindrical pipe. Where is the speed of the liquid layer maximum?","image":"","options":["At the walls of the pipe","Midway between the axis and the wall","Along the axis of the pipe","It is the same everywhere across the cross-section"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The layer in contact with the wall is at rest, because fluid touching a surface moves with that surface.\nThe speed increases gradually towards the centre, so it is maximum along the axis.\nThe speed is constant on any cylindrical surface coaxial with the pipe.\nOption (4) would hold only for an ideal, non-viscous fluid.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Water (density $1000$ kg m$^{-3}$) flows steadily through a horizontal pipe whose cross-section narrows from 40 cm² to 10 cm². The pressure in the wide part exceeds that in the narrow part by 15000 Pa. Treating water as ideal, the volume flow rate through the pipe is approximately:","image":"","options":["2.83 L/s","5.66 L/s","8.00 L/s","22.6 L/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $A_1 = 40\\times10^{-4}$ m², $A_2 = 10\\times10^{-4}$ m², $\\Delta P = 15000$ Pa.\nContinuity: $v_2 = \\frac{A_1}{A_2}v_1 = 4v_1$.\nBernoulli: $\\Delta P = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho(16v_1^2 - v_1^2) = 7500\\,v_1^2$\nSubstituting: $v_1^2 = \\frac{15000}{7500} = 2$, so $v_1 \\approx 1.414$ m/s.\nFlow rate: $Q = A_1v_1 = 40\\times10^{-4} \\times 1.414 \\approx 5.66\\times10^{-3}$ m³/s $= 5.66$ L/s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about capillary action are correct?\n(i) For the same liquid and tube material, the capillary rise is larger in a narrower tube.\n(ii) Water in a clean glass capillary has a concave meniscus.\n(iii) Just below the meniscus of water in a glass capillary, the pressure is greater than atmospheric pressure.\n(iv) If the angle of contact is obtuse, the liquid level inside the capillary is lower than outside.\n(v) The capillary rise does not depend on the density of the liquid.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $h \\propto \\frac{1}{a}$.\n(ii) Correct: the contact angle is acute, so the meniscus is concave.\n(iii) Incorrect: the pressure is less than atmospheric by $\\frac{2S\\cos\\theta}{a}$.\n(iv) Correct: $\\cos\\theta < 0$ makes h negative, so the liquid is depressed.\n(v) Incorrect: $h = \\frac{2S\\cos\\theta}{\\rho g a}$ depends on $\\rho$.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pressure unit) with List-II (approximate value in pascal).\nList-I:\n(a) 1 atm\n(b) 1 bar\n(c) 1 torr\n(d) 1 millibar\nList-II:\n(i) 100 Pa\n(ii) 133 Pa\n(iii) $1.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa\n(iv) $1.013 \\times 10^5$ Pa","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) 1 atm is the sea-level atmospheric pressure, $1.013 \\times 10^5$ Pa, so (a)-(iv).\n(b) 1 bar = $10^5$ Pa exactly, so (b)-(iii).\n(c) 1 torr = 1 mm of Hg = 133 Pa, so (c)-(ii).\n(d) 1 millibar = $10^{-3}$ bar = 100 Pa, so (d)-(i).\nThe common trap is swapping atm and bar, which are close but not equal.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Two identical clean glass capillary tubes stand vertically. One is dipped in a beaker of water and the other in a beaker of mercury. Which description of the two tubes is correct?","image":"","options":["Water: concave meniscus, level inside above the outside level; Mercury: convex meniscus, level inside below the outside level","Both show a concave meniscus, and the liquid rises in both tubes","Water: convex meniscus, level rises; Mercury: concave meniscus, level falls","Water: concave meniscus, level rises; Mercury: convex meniscus, level also rises"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The water–glass contact angle is acute, so the meniscus is concave. The pressure just below it is less than atmospheric, so water rises.\nThe mercury–glass contact angle is obtuse, so $\\cos\\theta$ is negative and the meniscus is convex. From $h = \\frac{2S\\cos\\theta}{\\rho g a}$, h is negative, so mercury is depressed.\nOption (4) fails because a convex meniscus produces a fall, not a rise.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"For a small sphere of radius $a$ moving through a viscous fluid, match List-I with List-II. In (B), (C) and (D), all other quantities are held constant.\nList-I\n(A) Coefficient of viscosity $\\eta$\n(B) Stokes' drag at a given speed\n(C) Terminal velocity\n(D) Weight of the sphere minus the buoyant force on it\nList-II\n(i) Proportional to $a^3$\n(ii) Proportional to $a$\n(iii) Dimensions $[ML^{-1}T^{-1}]$\n(iv) Proportional to $a^2$","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The SI unit of $\\eta$ is Pa s, so its dimensions are $[ML^{-1}T^{-1}]$: A-iii.\n(B) $F = 6\\pi\\eta a v$, so $F \\propto a$: B-ii.\n(C) $v_t = \\frac{2a^2(\\rho-\\sigma)g}{9\\eta}$, so $v_t \\propto a^2$: C-iv.\n(D) Weight minus buoyancy $= \\frac{4}{3}\\pi a^3(\\rho-\\sigma)g$, so it is $\\propto a^3$: D-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The force exerted by a fluid at rest on any surface in contact with it is always perpendicular to that surface.\nReason (R): If the force had a component parallel to the surface, the surface would exert an equal and opposite parallel force on the fluid by Newton's third law, and this force would make the fluid flow along the surface.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that a fluid at rest exerts only normal forces on surfaces.\nR is true. A tangential component would, by Newton's third law, produce a parallel reaction force on the fluid. That force would make the fluid flow.\nA fluid at rest cannot be flowing, so a tangential component is ruled out. R is therefore exactly the reason for A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Water (density $1000$ kg m$^{-3}$) flows steadily through a pipe. At point 1 the cross-section is 20 cm², the speed is 2 m/s and the pressure is $2.0 \\times 10^5$ Pa. Point 2 is 5 m higher than point 1 and has a cross-section of 10 cm². Treating water as ideal ($g = 10$ m/s²), the pressure at point 2 is:","image":"","options":["$1.44 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$1.50 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$1.56 \\times 10^5$ Pa","$1.94 \\times 10^5$ Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A_1 = 20$ cm², $A_2 = 10$ cm², $v_1 = 2$ m/s, $P_1 = 2.0\\times10^5$ Pa, $h_2 - h_1 = 5$ m.\nContinuity: $v_2 = \\frac{A_1 v_1}{A_2} = 4$ m/s.\nBernoulli: $P_2 = P_1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\rho(v_2^2 - v_1^2) - \\rho g(h_2 - h_1)$\nSubstituting: $P_2 = 200000 - 6000 - 50000$\nSo $P_2 = 1.44\\times10^5$ Pa.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A loaded aircraft of mass $2.0 \\times 10^5$ kg is in level flight. Its total wing area is 400 m$^2$, the average of the air speeds above and below the wings is 250 m/s, and the density of air is 1.25 kg/m$^3$. Neglecting the height difference across the wing, by roughly what percentage must the air speed over the upper surface exceed that under the lower surface? (Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$)","image":"","options":["3.2 %","6.4 %","8.0 %","12.8 %"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: m = $2.0\\times10^5$ kg, A = 400 m$^2$, $v_{av}$ = 250 m/s, $\\rho$ = 1.25 kg/m$^3$.\nLift balances weight: $\\Delta P = \\frac{mg}{A} = \\frac{2.0\\times10^6}{400} = 5000$ Pa.\nFormula: $\\Delta P = \\frac{\\rho}{2}(v_2+v_1)(v_2-v_1) = \\rho v_{av}(v_2-v_1)$, so $\\frac{v_2-v_1}{v_{av}} = \\frac{\\Delta P}{\\rho v_{av}^2}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{5000}{1.25 \\times 62500} = 0.064$.\nSo the upper-surface speed must be about 6.4 % higher. Dropping the factor of 2 relation would give 3.2 % or 12.8 %.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A soap bubble of radius 2.0 cm is blown in air. The surface tension of the soap solution is $0.030$ N/m. What is the excess pressure inside the bubble over the outside air?","image":"","options":["3.0 Pa","6.0 Pa","1.5 Pa","12 Pa"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $r = 2.0$ cm $= 0.020$ m, $S = 0.030$ N/m.\nFormula: A soap bubble has two liquid–air surfaces, inner and outer, so $P_i - P_o = \\frac{4S}{r}$.\nSubstituting: $P_i - P_o = \\frac{4 \\times 0.030}{0.020}$.\nSo the excess pressure $= 6.0$ Pa.\nThe tempting 3.0 Pa comes from using $\\frac{2S}{r}$, which applies to a drop or an air bubble, each with only one surface.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Efflux from a tank open to the atmosphere\n(B) Rocket propulsion\n(C) Magnus effect\n(D) Lift on an aeroplane wing\nList-II\n(I) Speed of efflux is set mainly by the container pressure, $v \\approx \\sqrt{\\frac{2(P-P_a)}{\\rho}}$\n(II) Spin drags air, crowding streamlines on one side of the ball\n(III) Speed of efflux equals that of a body falling freely through height h\n(IV) Shape and orientation crowd streamlines above, so air flows faster on top","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(III): With P = $P_a$, $v = \\sqrt{2gh}$, the free-fall speed (Torricelli's law).\n(B)-(I): When $P \\gg P_a$, the 2gh term is negligible and the container pressure decides the speed, as in rockets.\n(C)-(II): A spinning ball drags air with it, crowding streamlines above and rarefying them below, which gives a dynamic lift.\n(D)-(IV): An aerofoil crowds streamlines above the wing, so the faster flow on top gives lower pressure there and an upward lift.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"An open tank contains a 0.50 m deep layer of water (density 1000 kg m$^{-3}$). A 0.40 m thick layer of oil (density 800 kg m$^{-3}$) floats on top of the water, and the two liquids do not mix. Take $g = 10$ m/s$^2$. At what depth below the free surface of the oil is the gauge pressure equal to 6200 Pa?","image":"","options":["0.70 m","0.62 m","0.775 m","0.80 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: oil layer 0.40 m (800 kg m$^{-3}$), water below (1000 kg m$^{-3}$), required gauge pressure 6200 Pa.\nFormula: $P_2 - P_1 = \\rho g h$, applied to each layer in turn.\nThe gauge pressure at the oil–water boundary is $800 \\times 10 \\times 0.40 = 3200$ Pa. This is less than 6200 Pa, so the required point lies in the water.\nThe extra pressure needed is 6200 − 3200 = 3000 Pa, which needs a water depth of $\\frac{3000}{1000 \\times 10} = 0.30$ m.\nSo the depth is 0.40 + 0.30 = 0.70 m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Surface tension acts in the plane of the interface and can be expressed as force per unit length.\n(ii) The surface energy of a liquid surface does not depend on the material on the other side of the interface.\n(iii) The excess pressure inside a soap bubble of radius $r$ is $\\frac{4S}{r}$, whereas inside an air bubble of radius $r$ within a liquid it is $\\frac{2S}{r}$.\n(iv) The energy needed to bring a molecule from the interior to the surface is roughly half the energy needed to remove it from the liquid completely.\n(v) Gases also show surface tension, because they have free surfaces like liquids.","image":"","options":["5","4","3","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: surface tension is force per unit length, or energy per unit area, acting in the plane of the interface.\n(ii) Incorrect: interfacial energy depends on the materials on both sides of the interface.\n(iii) Correct: a soap bubble has two surfaces, giving $\\frac{4S}{r}$. An air bubble in a liquid has one surface, giving $\\frac{2S}{r}$.\n(iv) Correct: a surface molecule has about half the neighbours, so it needs about half the heat of evaporation per molecule.\n(v) Incorrect: gases have no free surface.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A small free drop of liquid becomes spherical when the effect of gravity can be neglected.\nStatement II: A soap bubble of radius r has twice the excess pressure of a liquid drop of the same radius and the same surface tension, because the bubble has two liquid–air surfaces.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. A liquid–air interface has surface energy. For a given volume, a sphere has the least surface area, so it has the minimum energy.\nStatement II: Correct. A drop has one surface, so its excess pressure is $\\frac{2S}{r}$. A soap bubble has two surfaces, so its excess pressure is $\\frac{4S}{r}$, which is twice as large.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A solid metal block has a mass of 540 g and a volume of 200 $cm^3$. Take the density of water at 4 °C as 1 g $cm^{-3}$. What is the relative density of the metal?","image":"","options":["0.37","2.7","27","2700"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: m = 540 g, V = 200 $cm^3$, $\\rho_{water}$ = 1 g $cm^{-3}$.\nFormula: $\\rho = \\frac{m}{V}$. Relative density = $\\frac{\\rho}{\\rho_{water}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\rho = \\frac{540}{200}$ = 2.7 g $cm^{-3}$, so relative density = 2.7/1.\nSo the relative density = 2.7. It is dimensionless, so 2700 (the density in kg $m^{-3}$) is wrong. This matches aluminium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In hydraulic brakes, a small force on the brake pedal produces a large retarding force on the wheel.\nStatement II: In hydraulic brakes, the braking effort is larger on the front wheels than on the rear wheels, because the front wheels are nearer to the master cylinder.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The pedal pushes the master piston, and the brake oil transmits the pressure to pistons of larger area. These pistons exert a large force on the brake shoes.\nStatement II is incorrect. Pressure is transmitted equally to all the wheel cylinders, whatever their distance from the master cylinder. NCERT notes that the braking effort is therefore equal on all four wheels.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is equal to 1 poiseuille (Pl), the SI unit of coefficient of viscosity?","image":"","options":["$1\\ \\text{N s m}^{-2}$","$1\\ \\text{N m}^{-2}\\text{ s}^{-1}$","$1\\ \\text{kg m s}^{-1}$","$1\\ \\text{N m s}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$\\eta = \\frac{F/A}{v/l}$, so its unit is $\\frac{\\text{N m}^{-2}}{\\text{s}^{-1}} = \\text{N s m}^{-2}$, which is the same as Pa s.\nNCERT names this unit the poiseuille (Pl).\nOption (2) divides by seconds instead of multiplying, so it is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Pressure\n(B) Density\n(C) Relative density\n(D) One atmosphere\nList-II\n(I) Dimensionless\n(II) $1.013 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa\n(III) $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$\n(IV) $[ML^{-3}]$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Pressure = force/area, with dimensions $[ML^{-1}T^{-2}]$, so A-III.\n(B) Density = mass/volume, with dimensions $[ML^{-3}]$, so B-IV.\n(C) Relative density is a ratio of two densities, so it is dimensionless: C-I.\n(D) 1 atm is the pressure of the atmosphere at sea level, $1.013 \\times 10^{5}$ Pa: D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"In the work-energy derivation of Bernoulli's equation, a volume $\\Delta V$ of fluid of density $\\rho$ moves from region 1 (area $A_1$, speed $v_1$, height $h_1$) to region 2 (area $A_2$, speed $v_2$, height $h_2$) in time $\\Delta t$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Work done on the fluid by pressure at the inlet end\n(B) Change in kinetic energy of the fluid\n(C) Change in gravitational potential energy of the fluid\n(D) Mass of fluid passing in time $\\Delta t$\nList-II\n(I) $\\rho g \\Delta V (h_2 - h_1)$\n(II) $P_1 A_1 v_1 \\Delta t$\n(III) $\\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\Delta V (v_2^2 - v_1^2)$\n(IV) $\\rho A_1 v_1 \\Delta t$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Work at the inlet = force × distance = $P_1A_1 \\times v_1\\Delta t$, which matches II.\n(B) $\\Delta K = \\frac{1}{2}\\Delta m(v_2^2 - v_1^2)$ with $\\Delta m = \\rho\\Delta V$, which matches III.\n(C) $\\Delta U = \\Delta m\\, g(h_2 - h_1) = \\rho g\\Delta V(h_2 - h_1)$, which matches I.\n(D) Mass = density × volume = $\\rho A_1 v_1\\Delta t$, which matches IV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-PHY-FLUIDS-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","type":"mcq","question":"A garden spray pump has a cylindrical tube of cross-sectional area $10\\ \\text{cm}^2$. Its nozzle end has 50 fine circular holes, each of diameter 0.8 mm. The liquid inside the tube moves at 2.4 m per minute. Assume steady flow of an incompressible liquid. What is the speed with which the liquid comes out of the holes?","image":"","options":["1.59 m/s","0.40 m/s","79.6 m/s","95.5 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A_1 = 10^{-3}\\ \\text{m}^2$, $v_1 = 2.4$ m/min $= 0.04$ m/s, hole radius $r = 0.4 \\times 10^{-3}$ m, n = 50.\nFormula: $A_1 v_1 = n\\pi r^2 v_2$.\nSubstituting: $v_2 = \\frac{10^{-3} \\times 0.04}{50 \\times \\pi (4 \\times 10^{-4})^2} = \\frac{4 \\times 10^{-5}}{2.51 \\times 10^{-5}}$.\nSo $v_2 \\approx 1.59$ m/s.\nUsing 0.8 mm as the radius gives 0.40 m/s, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Physics, Ch 9 · Mechanical Properties of Fluids","difficulty":"hard"}]});
